// --- COMPLETE WORKSHEET DATA (YEAR-WISE) ---
const worksheetData = [
    // 2025 Questions
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-1', type: 'brief', question: 'Q1: A wire of resistance R is cut into three equal parts. If these three parts are then joined in parallel, calculate the total resistance of the combination so formed.', answer: 'The total resistance is <strong><sup>R</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub></strong>. <br/><br/>Each part has resistance <sup>R</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>. In parallel: <br/> <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>Rₚ</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>(R/3)</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>(R/3)</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>(R/3)</sub> = <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>. <br/>So, Rₚ = <sup>R</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub>.', hint: 'Find the resistance of one part, then use the parallel resistance formula.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-2', type: 'brief', question: 'Q2: Define electric power. When do we say that the power consumed in an electric circuit is 1 watt?', answer: '<strong>Electric Power:</strong> The rate at which electrical energy is consumed in a circuit. <br/><strong>1 Watt:</strong> Power is 1 watt when 1 ampere of current flows through a circuit with a potential difference of 1 volt.', hint: 'Power is energy per unit time.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-3', type: 'long', question: 'Q3: <br/>(a) Write the relationship between resistivity and resistance of a cylindrical conductor of length l and area of cross-section A. Hence derive the SI unit of resistivity.<br/>(b) Why are alloys used in electrical heating devices?', answer: '<strong>(a) Relationship and SI Unit:</strong><br/>The relationship is <strong>R = ρ(<sup>l</sup>&frasl;<sub>A</sub>)</strong>. From this, ρ = <sup>(R × A)</sup>&frasl;<sub>l</sub>. <br/>The SI unit is derived as <sup>(Ohm × m²)</sup>&frasl;<sub>m</sub> = <strong>Ohm-meter (Ω·m)</strong>. <br/><br/><strong>(b) Use of Alloys:</strong><br/>Alloys are used because they have <strong>high resistivity</strong> (producing more heat) and <strong>do not oxidize or melt easily</strong> at high temperatures.', hint: 'For part (a), rearrange the formula to solve for resistivity and substitute the SI units.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-4', type: 'long', question: 'Q4: <br/>(a) Write the colours of the insulation covers of the line wires through which supply comes to our homes.<br/>(b) What should be the current rating of the electric circuit (220 V) so that an electric iron of 1 kW power rating can be operated?<br/>(c) What is the function of the earth wire? State the advantage of the earth wire in domestic electric appliances such as electric iron.', answer: '<strong>(a) Colours:</strong> According to the new international convention: Live wire: <strong>Brown</strong>, Neutral wire: <strong>Light Blue</strong>, Earth wire: <strong>Green or Yellow</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Current Rating:</strong> A <strong>5 A</strong> rating should be used. <br/>Current I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>1000 W</sup>&frasl;<sub>220 V</sub> ≈ 4.54 A. The fuse rating should be slightly higher than the operating current. The next standard value is 5 A.<br/><br/><strong>(c) Earth Wire Function:</strong> The earth wire is a safety measure that connects the metallic body of an appliance to the ground. If a live wire accidentally touches the metal casing, the earth wire provides a low-resistance path for the current to flow to the ground. This causes a large current to flow, which blows the fuse and disconnects the supply, preventing a severe electric shock.', hint: 'These colors are standardized for safety. Calculate current using I = P/V and choose the next standard fuse rating. The earth wire is a critical safety feature.'},
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-5', type: 'long', question: `Q5: Consider the following circuits. In which circuit will the power dissipated be (I) minimum (II) maximum? Justify your answer. <br/> <br/> <div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759035383/30de4d47-8250-434b-9650-0c358f719ba0.png' alt='Circuit diagrams for Q5' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div><div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div>Two lamps, rated 100 W; 220 V and 60 W; 220 V, are connected in parallel to an electric main supply of 220 V. Find the current drawn by the two lamps from the supply.`, answer: '<strong>For the first part:</strong><br/><strong>(I) Minimum power in circuit (ii)</strong>. <br/><strong>(II) Maximum power in circuit (iii)</strong>. <br/><strong>Justification:</strong> Power P = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>. Since V is constant, power is inversely proportional to resistance. <br/>- Circuit (ii) has the maximum resistance (5+5 = 10Ω), so it has minimum power. <br/>- Circuit (iii) has the minimum resistance (equivalent of two 5Ω resistors in parallel = 2.5Ω), so it has maximum power.<br/><br/><strong>For the OR part:</strong><br/>The total current is <strong>0.73 A</strong> (approx). <br/>I₁ = <sup>P₁</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>100</sup>&frasl;<sub>220</sub> A. <br/>I₂ = <sup>P₂</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>60</sup>&frasl;<sub>220</sub> A. <br/>Total current I = I₁ + I₂ = <sup>(100+60)</sup>&frasl;<sub>220</sub> = <sup>160</sup>&frasl;<sub>220</sub> ≈ 0.727 A.', hint: 'Power is inversely proportional to resistance when voltage is constant. In a parallel circuit, total current is the sum of currents in each branch.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-6', type: 'long', question: 'Q6: <br/>(a) Define one ampere. <br/>(b) The resistance of a wire of 0.01 cm radius is 14 Ω. If the resistivity of the material of the wire is 44 × 10⁻⁸ Ω m, find the length of the wire.', answer: '<strong>(a) One Ampere:</strong> One ampere is defined as the flow of one coulomb of charge through a conductor in one second (1 A = 1 C/s).<br/><br/><strong>(b) Length Calculation:</strong> The length of the wire is <strong>1 meter</strong>. <br/>Radius r = 0.01 cm = 10⁻⁴ m. <br/>Area A = πr² = (<sup>22</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>) × (10⁻⁴)² m². <br/>Length l = <sup>(R × A)</sup>&frasl;<sub>ρ</sub> = <sup>(14 × (<sup>22</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>) × 10⁻⁸)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(44 × 10⁻⁸)</sub> = 1 m.', hint: 'Use R = ρ(l/A) and ensure all units are in SI.'},
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-7', type: 'brief', question: 'Q7: Consider two lamps A and B of rating 50 W; 220 V and 25 W; 220 V respectively. Find the ratio of the resistances of the two lamps (i.e., Rₐ : Rₑ).', answer: 'The ratio Rₐ : Rₑ is <strong>1:2</strong>. <br/><br/>Using R = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>P</sub>: <br/>Rₐ = <sup>220²</sup>&frasl;<sub>50</sub> and Rₑ = <sup>220²</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub>. <br/>The ratio <sup>Rₐ</sup>&frasl;<sub>Rₑ</sub> = <sup>(220²/50)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(220²/25)</sub> = <sup>25</sup>&frasl;<sub>50</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>.', hint: 'Resistance is inversely proportional to power when voltage is the same.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-8', type: 'brief', question: 'Q8: Heat produced per second due to a current in a resistor of 4 Ω is 400 joules. Calculate the potential difference across the resistor.', answer: 'The potential difference is <strong>40 V</strong>. <br/><br/>Heat per second is Power, P = 400 W. <br/>Using P = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>, we get V² = P × R = 400 × 4 = 1600. <br/>V = 40 Volts.', hint: 'Heat produced per second is simply Power (in Watts).' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-9', type: 'long', question: 'Q9: <br/>(a) Define one volt potential difference between two points in an electric field. <br/>(b) Draw a schematic diagram of an electric circuit of a cell of 1.5 V, 5 Ω and 10 Ω resistors, and a plug key, all connected in series. Calculate the current drawn from the cell when the key is closed.', answer: '<strong>(a) One Volt:</strong> The potential difference between two points is said to be 1 volt if 1 joule of work is done to move a charge of 1 coulomb from one point to the other.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Diagram and Calculation:</strong><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759064386/33010663-a3b8-4700-9ce9-5958244ae913.png\' alt=\'Series circuit with 1.5V cell, 5Ω and 10Ω resistors, and a key\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div><br/>The current drawn is <strong>0.1 A</strong>. <br/>Total Resistance R_s = 5 Ω + 10 Ω = 15 Ω. <br/>Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_s</sub> = <sup>1.5 V</sup>&frasl;<sub>15 Ω</sub> = 0.1 A.', hint: 'For a series circuit, first find the total resistance.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-10', question: 'Q10: An electric bulb is connected to a power supply of 220 V. If the current drawn by the bulb from the supply is 500 mA, the power of the bulb is:', options: ['(a) 11 W', '(b) 110 W', '(c) 220 W', '(d) 1100 W'], answer: '(b) 110 W', hint: 'Use P = V × I. Convert mA to A by dividing by 1000.', explanation: 'Power (P) = Voltage (V) × Current (I).<br/>Given V = 220 V and I = 500 mA = 0.5 A.<br/>P = 220 V × 0.5 A = 110 W.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-11', question: `Q11: Four identical resistors of 12 Ω each are connected to form a square ABCD as shown. The resistance of the network between points 1 and 2 is: <br/> <br/> <div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759035453/95a41d42-86cc-427f-bec3-54fae20a2d2c.png' alt='Resistor square for Q11' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, options: ['(a) 48 Ω', '(b) 36 Ω', '(c) 9 Ω', '(d) 6 Ω'], answer: '(c) 9 Ω', hint: 'This is equivalent to finding resistance across adjacent corners. The path ADCB (3 resistors in series) is in parallel with the direct path AB.', explanation: 'When calculating resistance between adjacent corners (like 1 and 2, which are points A and B), the resistor between them (path AB, 12Ω) is in parallel with the other three resistors connected in series (path ADCB). <br/>Resistance of path ADCB = 12Ω + 12Ω + 12Ω = 36Ω. <br/>The equivalent resistance R_eq is found by: <br/><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_eq</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>36</sub> = <sup>(3+1)</sup>&frasl;<sub>36</sub> = <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>36</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub>. <br/>So, R_eq = 9 Ω.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-12', question: 'Q12: Assertion (A): Nichrome is an alloy commonly used in electrical heating devices. <br/>Reason (R): The resistivity of nichrome is high, and its resistance decreases with an increase in temperature.', options: ['(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.', '(b) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.', '(c) A is true, but R is false.', '(d) A is false, but R is true.'], answer: '(c) A is true, but R is false.', hint: 'Resistance of metallic conductors/alloys increases with temperature.', explanation: 'Assertion (A) is true; nichrome is an ideal material for heating elements due to its high resistivity. Reason (R) is false because the resistance of alloys like nichrome (and most metallic conductors) increases with an increase in temperature.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-13', type: 'brief', question: 'Q13: An electric kettle is rated 750 W; 220 V. Can this kettle be used in a circuit which has a fuse of current rating 3 A? Give reason.', answer: '<strong>No.</strong><br/><br/>Current drawn I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>750 W</sup>&frasl;<sub>220 V</sub> ≈ 3.41 A. Since 3.41 A > 3 A, the fuse will blow.', hint: 'Calculate the current the kettle draws and compare it to the fuse rating.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-14', type: 'long', question: 'Q14: Three resistors of 2 Ω, 3 Ω, and 6 Ω are connected in (i) series, and (ii) parallel. Draw the arrangements and find the equivalent resistance of each.', answer: '<strong>(i) Series:</strong> R_s = 2 + 3 + 6 = <strong>11 Ω</strong>.<br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759065533/c7f31a18-a9cd-4f59-96fd-3141564913f0.png\' alt=\'Series connection for Q14\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div><br/><br/><strong>(ii) Parallel:</strong> <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_p</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = <sup>(3+2+1)</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = 1. So, R_p = <strong>1 Ω</strong>.<br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759065415/a8262acb-0b90-44e1-97a0-ddcec1034696.png\' alt=\'Parallel connection for Q14\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div>', hint: 'Use the respective formulas for series and parallel combinations.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-15', question: 'Q15: The resistivity of a wire made of an alloy is generally:', options: ['(a) Lower than that of its constituent metals.', '(b) Higher than that of its constituent metals.', '(c) Decreases with an increase in its area of cross-section.', '(d) Increases with an increase in its length.'], answer: '(b) Higher than that of its constituent metals.', hint: 'Resistivity is a material property, independent of length or area.', explanation: 'Alloys have a more disordered atomic structure compared to their pure constituent metals. This increased disorder impedes the flow of electrons, resulting in a higher electrical resistivity. Resistivity is an intrinsic property of a material and does not change with the dimensions (length or area) of the wire.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-16', type: 'long', question: 'Q16: Resistance of a wire of length 1 m is 35 Ω at 20°C. If the diameter of the wire is 0.2 mm, determine the resistivity of the material. How will the resistivity change if length and diameter are doubled?', answer: 'The resistivity is <strong>1.1 × 10⁻⁶ Ω·m</strong>. The resistivity will <strong>not change</strong>. <br/><br/>Radius r = 0.1mm = 10⁻⁴ m. Area A = πr² = (<sup>22</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>) × 10⁻⁸ m². <br/>ρ = <sup>(R × A)</sup>&frasl;<sub>l</sub> = <sup>(35 × (<sup>22</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>) × 10⁻⁸)</sup>&frasl;<sub>1</sub> = 110 × 10⁻⁸ = 1.1 × 10⁻⁶ Ω·m. <br/><br/>Resistivity is an intrinsic property of the material; it does not depend on the wire\'s dimensions.', hint: 'Resistivity depends on the material, not its shape or size.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-17', type: 'long', question: 'Q17: How and why is an electric fuse used in an electric circuit? Briefly describe its function.', answer: '<strong>How:</strong> A fuse is always connected in <strong>series</strong> with the live wire. <br/><strong>Why/Function:</strong> It is a safety device made of a wire with a low melting point. If the current exceeds a safe limit, the wire melts due to the heating effect (H=I²Rt), breaking the circuit and protecting appliances from damage.', hint: 'It acts as a deliberate weak link in the circuit.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-18', question: 'Q18: A wire of length \'l\' is gradually stretched so that its length increases to 3l. If its original resistance is R, then its new resistance will be:', options: ['(a) 3R', '(b) 6R', '(c) 9R', '(d) 27R'], answer: '(c) 9R', hint: 'When a wire is stretched, its volume is constant. If length becomes 3l, area becomes A/3.', explanation: 'When a wire is stretched, its volume (V = Area × length) remains constant. If the new length l\' = 3l, the new area A\' must be A/3 to maintain the same volume. The original resistance is R = ρ(l/A). The new resistance R\' = ρ(l\'/A\') = ρ((3l)/(A/3)) = 9 × ρ(l/A) = 9R.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-19', type: 'brief', question: 'Q19: An electric kettle is rated 230 V; 1000 W. Calculate the resistance of its heating element when in operation.', answer: 'The resistance is <strong>52.9 Ω</strong>. <br/><br/>Using R = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>P</sub> = <sup>(230)²</sup>&frasl;<sub>1000</sub> = <sup>52900</sup>&frasl;<sub>1000</sub> = 52.9 Ω.', hint: 'Use the power formula that relates P, V, and R.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-20', type: 'brief', question: 'Q20: A voltage source sends a current of 2 A to a resistor of 40 Ω connected across it for 5 minutes. Calculate the electrical energy supplied by the source.', answer: 'The energy supplied is <strong>48,000 J</strong> or <strong>48 kJ</strong>. <br/><br/>Energy E = I²Rt. Time t = 5 min = 300 s. <br/>E = (2)² × 40 × 300 = 4 × 40 × 300 = 48000 J.<br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759065864/9f787204-2831-4979-807e-826dbd535dbd.png\' alt=\'Energy calculation illustration\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div>', hint: 'Use the formula for electrical energy (Joule\'s law) and convert time to seconds.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-21', question: 'Q21: In domestic electric circuits, the colour of insulation covers of wires in the cables of electric iron/electric toaster is generally:', options: ['(a) red for live wire, green for neutral wire, black for earth wire', '(b) red for live wire, black for neutral wire, green for earth wire', '(c) green for live wire, black for neutral wire, red for earth wire', '(d) green for live wire, red for neutral wire, black for earth wire'], answer: '(b) red for live wire, black for neutral wire, green for earth wire', hint: 'This is based on the old convention common in India.', explanation: 'This question refers to the old color coding convention traditionally used in India and other regions. In this system: Live wire is Red, Neutral wire is Black, and Earth wire is Green. It is important to also know the new international convention: Live is Brown, Neutral is Light Blue, and Earth is Green or Yellow.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-22', type: 'brief', question: `Q22: Four resistors, each of resistance 2.0 Ω, are joined to form a square ABCD. Determine the equivalent resistance between ends A and B. <br/> <br/> <div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759035595/651d2330-37f7-4d7a-a271-7c033ff43c41.png' alt='Resistor square for Q22' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: 'The equivalent resistance is <strong>1.5 Ω</strong>. <br/><br/>The path ADCB is a series combination (2+2+2 = 6 Ω). This path is in parallel with the direct path AB (2 Ω). <br/><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_eq</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub>. So, R_eq = <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = 1.5 Ω.', hint: 'Identify the series and parallel paths between the two points.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-23', question: 'Q23: A piece of wire of resistance \'R\' is cut lengthwise into three identical parts. These are then connected in parallel. If the equivalent resistance is R\', find R/R\'.', options: ['(a) 1/9', '(b) 1/3', '(c) 3', '(d) 9'], answer: '(d) 9', hint: `The phrase 'cut lengthwise' can be ambiguous. The most common interpretation in such problems means cutting the wire into three shorter pieces of equal length. In this case, each part has resistance R/3. In parallel, R' = (R/3)/3 = R/9. Thus, <sup>R</sup>&frasl;<sub>R'</sub> = 9.`, explanation: 'When the wire of resistance R is cut into three identical parts, each part will have a resistance of R/3. When these three parts are connected in parallel, the equivalent resistance R\' is given by the formula: <br/><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R\'</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>(R/3)</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>(R/3)</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>(R/3)</sub> = <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> + <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> + <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>. <br/>Therefore, R\' = R/9. The ratio R/R\' is R / (R/9) = 9.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-24', question: 'Q24: An electric bulb is rated 220 V; 11 W. The resistance of its filament is:', options: ['(a) 4400 Ω', '(b) 440 Ω', '(c) 400 Ω', '(d) 20 Ω'], answer: '(a) 4400 Ω', hint: 'Use P = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>. R = <sup>(220×220)</sup>&frasl;<sub>11</sub> = 4400 Ω.', explanation: 'The relationship between power (P), voltage (V), and resistance (R) is P = V²/R. We can rearrange this to solve for resistance: R = V²/P. <br/>Given V = 220 V and P = 11 W. <br/>R = (220 V)² / 11 W = 48400 / 11 = 4400 Ω.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-25', question: 'Q25: The minimum number of identical bulbs of rating 4 V; 6 W that can work safely in series with a 240 V mains supply is:', options: ['(a) 20', '(b) 40', '(c) 60', '(d) 80'], answer: '(c) 60', hint: 'Total voltage rating of bulbs must match mains voltage. Number = <sup>240V</sup>&frasl;<sub>4V</sub> = 60.', explanation: 'For bulbs connected in series to work safely, the sum of their individual voltage ratings must be equal to the mains supply voltage. <br/>Voltage of mains supply = 240 V. <br/>Voltage rating of one bulb = 4 V. <br/>Minimum number of bulbs required = (Mains Voltage) / (Bulb Voltage) = 240 V / 4 V = 60.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-26', type: 'long', question: `Q26: <br/>(i) Classify them as conductor, alloy, and insulator. <br/>(ii) Give an example and use for each.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759035603/96b97f7c-26b3-49d2-99ca-f77786cc4775.png" alt="Resistivity Table" class="mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image" /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(i) Classification:</strong><br/>- A: <strong>Insulator</strong> (very high resistivity)<br/>- B: <strong>Alloy</strong> (medium resistivity)<br/>- C: <strong>Conductor</strong> (very low resistivity)<br/><br/><strong>(ii) Examples & Uses:</strong><br/>- A: Rubber, used for wire insulation.<br/>- B: Nichrome, used for heating elements in toasters.<br/>- C: Copper, used for connecting wires.', hint: 'The value of resistivity determines the material type.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-27', type: 'brief', question: 'Q27: An electric fuse is rated 8 A. Can it be used with an appliance of rating 5 kW, 200 V? Justify.', answer: '<strong>No.</strong><br/><br/>Current drawn I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>5000 W</sup>&frasl;<sub>200 V</sub> = 25 A. Since 25 A > 8 A, the fuse will blow.', hint: 'Calculate the current needed by the appliance.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-28', type: 'long', question: 'Q28: Define "potential difference". Name and define its S.I. unit. Express it in terms of S.I. unit of work and charge.', answer: '<strong>Potential Difference:</strong> The work done to move a unit positive charge from one point to another. <br/><strong>S.I. Unit:</strong> Volt (V). <br/><strong>Definition:</strong> 1 Volt is the potential difference when 1 Joule of work is done to move 1 Coulomb of charge. <br/><strong>Expression:</strong> 1 V = <sup>1 J</sup>&frasl;<sub>1 C</sub>.', hint: 'Potential difference is work done per unit charge.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-29', type: 'long', question: 'Q29: <br/>(a) State two applications of Joule\'s heating. <br/>(b) Establish the relationship between the commercial and SI unit of energy <div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div> determine the total resistance of 2Ω, 4Ω and 6Ω in parallel.', answer: '<strong>(a) Applications of Joule\'s Heating:</strong> <br/>1. Electric heaters, toasters, irons. <br/>2. Electric fuse for safety. <br/><br/><strong>(b) (i) Energy Units Relationship:</strong><br/>1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = <strong>3.6 × 10⁶ J</strong>. <br/><br/><strong>(OR) (ii) Parallel Resistance:</strong><br/><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_p</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = <sup>(6+3+2)</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub> = <sup>11</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>. <br/>R_p = <strong><sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>11</sub> Ω ≈ 1.09 Ω</strong>.', hint: 'Joule heating is the principle that current produces heat.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-30', type: 'long', question: 'Q30: The resistance of a wire of 0.01 cm radius is 7 Ω. If the resistivity is 44 × 10⁻⁶ Ω·m, calculate the length of the wire.', answer: 'The length is <strong>0.005 m (or 5 mm)</strong>. <br/><br/>Radius r = 10⁻⁴ m. Area A = πr² = (<sup>22</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>) × 10⁻⁸ m². <br/>l = <sup>(R × A)</sup>&frasl;<sub>ρ</sub> = <sup>(7 × (<sup>22</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>) × 10⁻⁸)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(44 × 10⁻⁶)</sub> = <sup>(22 × 10⁻⁸)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(44 × 10⁻⁶)</sub> = 0.5 × 10⁻² m.', hint: 'Be careful with powers of 10 during calculation.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-31', type: 'long', question: 'Q31: <br/>(a) Justify the importance of the earth wire. <br/>(b) List two precautions to avoid overloading.', answer: '<strong>(a) Earth Wire Importance:</strong> It is a safety wire. If a fault causes the live wire to touch the metal body of an appliance, the earth wire provides a safe path for current to flow to the ground, blowing the fuse and preventing electric shock. <br/><br/><strong>(b) Precautions for Overloading:</strong> <br/>1. Do not connect too many high-power appliances to a single socket. <br/>2. Use appropriate fuses/MCBs for each circuit.', hint: 'Overloading means drawing more current than a circuit is designed for.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-32', type: 'long', question: `Q32: <br/>(A) Use Ohm’s law to determine the potential difference across the 6 Ω resistor in the circuit shown. <br/> <br/> <div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036104/4763d0ec-3cba-49d7-bed3-8658a1fe3b09.png' alt='Series circuit for Q32' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div><div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div>(B) Prove that if the current through a resistor is increased by 100%, then the increase in power dissipated will be 300%.`, answer: '<strong>(A) Potential Difference:</strong> The potential difference is <strong>3 V</strong>. <br/>Total Resistance R_s = 2+6+4 = 12 Ω. <br/>Total Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_s</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>12Ω</sub> = 0.5 A. <br/>Voltage across 6Ω resistor V₆ = I × R₆ = 0.5 A × 6 Ω = 3 V.<br/><br/><strong>(B) OR - Proof:</strong> Let initial current = I. Initial power P₁ = I²R.<br/>New current I₂ = I + 100% of I = 2I. <br/>New power P₂ = (2I)²R = 4I²R. <br/>Increase in power = P₂ - P₁ = 3I²R. <br/>Percentage Increase = (<sup>Increase</sup>&frasl;<sub>Original</sub>) × 100% = (<sup>3I²R</sup>&frasl;<sub>I²R</sub>) × 100% = <strong>300%</strong>.', hint: 'Find the total current first for part (A). For part (B), remember that an increase of 100% means the value doubles.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-33', type: 'long', question: 'Q33: What is short circuiting? State its possible causes. What is likely to happen if a domestic circuit gets short circuited? Give reason.', answer: '<strong>Short Circuiting:</strong> This happens when the live wire and neutral wire come into direct contact. <br/><strong>Causes:</strong> Damaged insulation, faults in appliances. <br/><strong>Consequences:</strong> The circuit resistance becomes almost zero, causing a very large current to flow. This produces immense heat (Joule\'s Law), which can damage appliances and cause fires. The fuse blows to prevent this.', hint: 'Short circuit = low resistance path.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-34', type: 'long', question: 'Q34: Determine the maximum and minimum resistance which can be obtained by joining five resistors of 1/5 Ω each.<div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div>Calculate potential difference across a 4 Ω resistor that produces 100 J of heat every second.', answer: '<strong>For the first part:</strong><br/><strong>Maximum resistance (in series) = 1 Ω.</strong> <br/>R_max = 5 × (<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub>) = 1 Ω. <br/><strong>Minimum resistance (in parallel) = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub> Ω.</strong> <br/><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_min</sub> = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>(1/5)</sub> = 25. So, R_min = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub> Ω.<br/><br/><strong>For the OR part:</strong><br/>The potential difference is <strong>20 V</strong>. <br/>Power P = 100 J/s = 100 W. <br/>V² = P × R = 100 × 4 = 400. <br/>V = 20 Volts.', hint: 'Maximum resistance is series, minimum is parallel. For the OR part, heat per second is power (P = V²/R).' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-35', question: 'Q35: Assertion (A): In common domestic circuits, the earth wire is connected to a metallic plate buried deep inside the earth. <br/>Reason (R): The earth wire ensures that any leakage of current to the metallic body of the appliance keeps its potential to that of the earth, so the user may not get a severe electric shock.', options: ['(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.', '(b) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.', '(c) A is true, but R is false.', '(d) A is false, but R is true.'], answer: '(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.', hint: 'The reason directly explains the safety purpose of the assertion.', explanation: 'Assertion (A) is the correct description of the earthing process. Reason (R) provides the correct scientific explanation for why earthing is a crucial safety measure. It keeps the appliance\'s body at earth potential (zero potential), providing a safe path for fault current and preventing electric shock. Thus, R is the correct explanation for A.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-36', type: 'long', question: `Q36: <br/>(a) Explain the statement "Potential difference between two points is 1 volt". <br/>(b) What do the symbols given below represent? Write one function of each.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759035617/67a4b203-1d10-41ba-8070-71c5b2da331f.png' alt='Circuit symbols for Q36' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(a) 1 Volt:</strong> This means 1 Joule of work is done to move a charge of 1 Coulomb between the two points. <br/><br/><strong>(b) Symbols:</strong><br/>- (i) [A] is an <strong>Ammeter</strong>. Function: Measures current in series. <br/>- (ii) Arrow through resistor is a <strong>Rheostat/Variable Resistor</strong>. Function: To vary the resistance in the circuit.', hint: 'Think about how each device is connected in a circuit.' },
    { section: '2025', id: '2025-37', type: 'long', question: `Q37: For a series circuit with resistors X=3Ω, Y=6Ω and a 2V battery: <br/>(I) Draw the parallel circuit diagram. <br/>(II) In which combination will V and I be the same across X & Y? <br/>(III) Find the current in the series combination.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759035625/6e45d61f-a9d6-4119-a561-887954d52d22.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q37' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(I) Parallel Diagram:</strong> [Image Holder: Parallel circuit with 2V battery, 3Ω and 6Ω resistors]. <br/><br/><strong>(II) Same V and I:</strong> <br/>- (i) Potential difference (V) is the same in a <strong>parallel</strong> combination. <br/>- (ii) Current (I) is the same in a <strong>series</strong> combination. <br/><br/><strong>(III) Current in Series:</strong> <br/>Total Resistance R_s = 3Ω + 6Ω = 9Ω. <br/>Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_s</sub> = <sup>2V</sup>&frasl;<sub>9Ω</sub> ≈ <strong>0.22 A</strong>.', hint: 'Remember the basic rules for series and parallel circuits.' },

            // 2024 Questions
            { section: '2024', id: '2024-1', type: 'long', question: `Q1: <br/>(A) Show how you would connect three resistors each of resistance 6 Ω, so that the combination has a resistance of 9 Ω. Justify your answer.<div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div>(B) In the given circuit calculate the power consumed in watts in the resistor of 2 Ω.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036265/51ba9cec-6d82-4e3f-ad4b-e690200bf5f1.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q1B' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(A) Connection for 9Ω:</strong> Connect two 6Ω resistors in parallel, and then connect the third 6Ω resistor in series with this parallel combination.<br/><strong>Justification:</strong> The two parallel resistors have an equivalent resistance Rₚ = <sup>(6×6)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(6+6)</sub> = 3Ω. This 3Ω is in series with the third 6Ω resistor, so the total resistance is 3Ω + 6Ω = <strong>9Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(B) Power Calculation:</strong> The power consumed is <strong>8 W</strong>. <br/>Total resistance R_s = 1 Ω + 2 Ω = 3 Ω. <br/>Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_s</sub> = <sup>6 V</sup>&frasl;<sub>3 Ω</sub> = 2 A. <br/>Power in 2 Ω resistor P = I²R = (2)² × 2 = <strong>8 W</strong>.', hint: 'For (A), experiment with series/parallel combinations. For (B), find the total current first.' },
            { section: '2024', id: '2024-2', type: 'long', question: 'Q2: <br/>(A)(i) Define electric power. Express it in terms of V and R. (ii) An oven (220V) consumes 11 units in 5 hours. Calculate: (a) power rating, (b) current, (c) resistance.<div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div>(B)(i) Write the relation between R and ρ. Derive SI unit of ρ. (ii) A 3m wire has 60Ω resistance and 4×10⁻⁷ m² area. Calculate ρ. (iii) What happens to ρ if the wire is stretched to double its length?', answer: '<strong>(A) Power and Oven Calculation:</strong><br/>(i) <strong>Electric Power</strong> is the rate of consumption of electrical energy. P = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>.<br/>(ii) Energy = 11 units = 11 kWh. Power = <sup>Energy</sup>&frasl;<sub>Time</sub> = <sup>11kWh</sup>&frasl;<sub>5h</sub> = 2.2 kW = <strong>2200 W</strong>.<br/>(b) Current I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>2200W</sup>&frasl;<sub>220V</sub> = <strong>10 A</strong>.<br/>(c) Resistance R = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub> = <sup>220V</sup>&frasl;<sub>10A</sub> = <strong>22 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(B) Resistivity Calculation:</strong><br/>(i) R = ρ(<sup>l</sup>&frasl;<sub>A</sub>). SI unit of ρ is <strong>Ohm-meter (Ω·m)</strong>.<br/>(ii) ρ = <sup>(R×A)</sup>&frasl;<sub>l</sub> = <sup>(60Ω × 4×10⁻⁷ m²)</sup>&frasl;<sub>3m</sub> = <strong>80 × 10⁻⁷ Ω·m</strong>.<br/>(iii) Resistivity (ρ) will <strong>remain unchanged</strong> as it is a property of the material, not its dimensions.', hint: '1 unit of energy is 1 kWh. Resistivity is an intrinsic property.' },
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-3', question: `Q3: Study the I-V graph for three resistors R₁, R₂, and R₃ and select the correct statement.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036299/28aaa424-a2d6-42ba-8132-60a089433e7f.png' alt='I-V graph for Q3' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, options: ['(a) R₁ = R₂ = R₃', '(b) R₁ > R₂ > R₃', '(c) R₃ > R₂ > R₁', '(d) R₂ > R₃ > R₁'], answer: '(c) R₃ > R₂ > R₁', hint: 'Resistance R = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub>. The slope of an I-V graph is <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>. A steeper slope means lower resistance.', explanation: 'The graph plots Current (I) on the y-axis and Voltage (V) on the x-axis. The slope of this I-V graph is I/V. According to Ohm\'s Law, R = V/I, so the slope is equal to 1/R. This means that a steeper slope corresponds to a smaller resistance. Since the slope of line R₁ is the steepest and the slope of line R₃ is the least steep, their resistances are in the order R₁ < R₂ < R₃.'},
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-4', type: 'brief', question: `Q4: Use Ohm’s law to determine the potential difference across the 3 Ω resistor in the circuit shown.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036305/c7dfeb5c-8e5c-4fb0-85b6-b25cdb905a13.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q4' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: 'The potential difference is <strong>1.0 V</strong>.<br/><br/>Total Resistance R_s = 1Ω + 2Ω + 3Ω = 6Ω.<br/>Total Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_s</sub> = <sup>2V</sup>&frasl;<sub>6Ω</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> A.<br/>Voltage across 3Ω resistor V₃ = I × R₃ = (<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> A) × 3Ω = 1 V.', hint: 'Find the total current in the series circuit first.'},
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-5', question: 'Q5: In the case of four wires of the same material, the resistance will be minimum if the diameter and length of the wire respectively are:', options: ['(a) D/2 and L/4', '(b) D/4 and 4L', '(c) 2D and L', '(d) 4D and 2L'], answer: '(d) 4D and 2L', hint: 'Resistance R = ρ(<sup>l</sup>&frasl;<sub>A</sub>). To minimize R, we need the largest area (largest diameter) and shortest length. Compare <sup>l</sup>&frasl;<sub>D²</sub> for all options.', explanation: 'Resistance (R) is directly proportional to length (l) and inversely proportional to the cross-sectional area (A), so R ∝ l/A. Since Area A is proportional to the square of the diameter (D²), we have R ∝ l/D². To find the minimum resistance, we must find the option with the smallest l/D² ratio. <br/>(a) (L/4)/(D/2)² = L/D². <br/>(b) 4L/(D/4)² = 64(L/D²). <br/>(c) L/(2D)² = (1/4)(L/D²). <br/>(d) 2L/(4D)² = (2/16)(L/D²) = (1/8)(L/D²). <br/>The smallest value is from option (d).'},
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-6', type: 'long', question: 'Q6: Explain the function of an electric fuse. An electric heater of 3 kW; 220 V is operated in a circuit of rating 5 A. What is likely to happen? Justify.', answer: '<strong>Fuse Function:</strong> A fuse is a safety device with a low melting point wire. It is connected in series. If current exceeds a safe limit, the wire melts, breaking the circuit and protecting appliances.<br/><br/><strong>Heater Scenario:</strong> The heater will <strong>not work and the fuse will blow</strong>.<br/><strong>Justification:</strong> Current drawn by heater I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>3000W</sup>&frasl;<sub>220V</sub> ≈ 13.64 A. Since 13.64 A is much greater than the 5 A fuse rating, the fuse will blow instantly.', hint: 'Calculate the current the heater needs to operate.'},
            { section: '2024', id: '2024-7', type: 'long', question: `Q7: <br/>(a) State Ohm’s law. Write the formula for equivalent resistance R of a parallel combination of R₁, R₂, and R₃.<br/>(b) Find the resistance of the network shown.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036345/5cc8e15b-47f3-4627-89e6-7ba589966d6e.png' alt='Resistor network for Q7' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(a) Ohm\'s Law:</strong> At constant temperature, current (I) is directly proportional to potential difference (V). Formula for parallel resistance: <strong><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₁</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₂</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₃</sub></strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Network Resistance:</strong> The total resistance is <strong>1.5R</strong>.<br/>The two resistors R in parallel give an equivalent resistance Rₚ = <sup>R</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>. This is in series with the first resistor R. So, total resistance = R + <sup>R</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = <strong><sup>3R</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> or 1.5R</strong>.', hint: 'Solve the parallel part of the circuit first, then the series part.'},
            { section: '2024', id: '2024-8', type: 'long', question: `Q8: Case-based question on domestic circuit.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036353/7756837b-f62b-40fc-a2c6-fbe5adedf250.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q8' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div><br/>(a) What happens when (i) K₁ is closed? (ii) K₂ is closed?<br/>(b) Find the current drawn by bulb B.<br/>(c) Calculate (i) resistance of bulb B, and (ii) total resistance of bulbs B, C, D, E.<div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div>(c) What happens if K₁ and K₂ are closed and bulb C fuses?`, answer: '<strong>(a)(i) K₁ closed:</strong> Bulb A (22W, 220V) will glow.<br/><strong>(a)(ii) K₂ closed:</strong> Bulbs B, C, D, E (all 11W, 55V) will glow.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Current by B:</strong> I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>11W</sup>&frasl;<sub>55V</sub> = <strong>0.2 A</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(c)(i) Resistance of B:</strong> R = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub> = <sup>55V</sup>&frasl;<sub>0.2A</sub> = <strong>275 Ω</strong>.<br/><strong>(c)(ii) Total Resistance of B,C,D,E:</strong> The four bulbs are in series. Total R = 4 × 275Ω = <strong>1100 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>OR (c):</strong> If bulb C fuses, the series circuit containing B, C, D, E breaks. <strong>Bulbs B, D, and E will stop glowing</strong>. Bulb A will remain unaffected and continue to glow.', hint: 'Fusing means the filament breaks, creating an open circuit.'},
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-9', question: `Q9: Consider the following combinations of resistors. The combinations having equivalent resistance 1 Ω is/are:<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036409/f06849a6-dc01-444c-96d2-909664f0239c.png' alt='Four resistor combinations for Q9' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, options: ['(a) I and IV', '(b) Only IV', '(c) I and II', '(d) I, II and III'], answer: '(c) I and II', hint: 'Calculate the equivalent resistance for each combination. I: 2Ω || 2Ω = 1Ω. II: 3Ω || 3Ω || 3Ω = 1Ω.', explanation: 'Let\'s calculate the equivalent resistance (R_eq) for each combination: <br/>(I) Two 2Ω resistors in parallel: R_eq = (2×2)/(2+2) = 1Ω. <br/>(II) Three 3Ω resistors in parallel: 1/R_eq = 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 = 1, so R_eq = 1Ω. <br/>(III) 1Ω and 2Ω in series: R_eq = 1 + 2 = 3Ω. <br/>(IV) Two 2Ω in series (4Ω) in parallel with 2Ω: R_eq = (4×2)/(4+2) = 8/6 = 4/3 Ω. <br/>Therefore, only combinations I and II result in an equivalent resistance of 1Ω.'},
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-10', question: 'Q10: An electric iron of resistance 20Ω draws a current of 5 A. The heat developed in the iron in 30 seconds is:', options: ['(a) 15000 J', '(b) 6000 J', '(c) 1500 J', '(d) 3000 J'], answer: '(a) 15000 J', hint: 'Use Joule\'s Law of Heating, H = I²Rt.', explanation: 'The heat developed is calculated using Joule\'s Law of Heating: H = I²Rt. <br/>Given: I = 5 A, R = 20 Ω, t = 30 s. <br/>H = (5 A)² × 20 Ω × 30 s = 25 × 20 × 30 = 15000 J.' },
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-11', type: 'brief', question: 'Q11: Two wires A and B of the same material and length have diameters 0.2 mm and 0.3 mm. Which will offer more resistance?', answer: '<strong>Wire A (diameter 0.2 mm) will offer more resistance.</strong><br/><br/><strong>Justification:</strong> Resistance R is inversely proportional to the area of cross-section (A = πd²/4). Since Wire A has a smaller diameter, it has a smaller cross-sectional area and therefore higher resistance.', hint: 'Resistance is inversely proportional to area.'},
            { section: '2024', id: '2024-12', type: 'long', question: `Q12: Case-based question on the circuit shown.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036441/394a6909-c413-41ac-8d99-00e29926a38b.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q12' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div><br/>(a) Find total resistance between A and B.<br/>(b) Find resistance between B and C.<br/>(c)(i) Calculate current drawn from the battery.<div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div>(c)(ii) Which has more potential difference: the 16Ω resistor or the parallel combination of 8Ω resistors?`, answer: '<strong>(a) Resistance A-B:</strong> The three resistors are in series. R_AB = 4Ω + 6Ω + 16Ω = <strong>26 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Resistance B-C:</strong> The two 8Ω resistors are in parallel. R_BC = <sup>(8×8)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(8+8)</sub> = <sup>64</sup>&frasl;<sub>16</sub> = <strong>4 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(c)(i) Current:</strong> Total resistance R_total = 26Ω + 4Ω = 30Ω. Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_total</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>30Ω</sub> = <strong>0.2 A</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>OR (c)(ii):</strong> The <strong>16Ω resistor has more potential difference</strong>.<br/><strong>Justification:</strong> V=IR. Current I=0.2A is the same for both. V₁₆ = 0.2A × 16Ω = 3.2V. V_parallel = 0.2A × 4Ω = 0.8V. Thus, 3.2V > 0.8V.', hint: 'Solve the circuit step-by-step: series part, parallel part, then total.'},
            { section: '2024', id: '2024-13', type: 'brief', question: 'Q13: An electric source can supply a charge of 500 coulomb. If the current drawn is 25 mA, find the time in which the source will be discharged.', answer: 'The source will be discharged in <strong>20,000 seconds</strong> (or 5.55 hours).<br/><br/>Current I = 25 mA = 0.025 A.<br/>Time t = <sup>Q</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub> = <sup>500 C</sup>&frasl;<sub>0.025 A</sub> = <strong>20000 s</strong>.', hint: 'Use the formula Q = I × t and convert mA to A.'},
            { section: '2024', id: '2024-14', type: 'long', question: 'Q14: <br/>(a)(i) If V is decreased to one-third, what happens to I? State the law. (ii) Draw a circuit with four 1.5V cells in series with 5Ω, 10Ω, 15Ω resistors. Find the total current and V across 10Ω.<div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div>(b)(i) When is potential difference 1 volt? (ii) A copper wire (ρ=1.6×10⁻⁸ Ωm) has 0.2mm diameter and 14Ω resistance. Find its length. What happens to R if diameter is doubled?', answer: '<strong>(a)(i):</strong> The current will also <strong>decrease to one-third</strong>. This is based on <strong>Ohm\'s Law</strong>, which states V ∝ I.<br/><strong>(a)(ii):</strong> Total V = 4 × 1.5V = 6V. Total R = 5+10+15 = 30Ω. Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>30Ω</sub> = <strong>0.2 A</strong>. Potential difference across 10Ω is V₁₀ = I×R₁₀ = 0.2A × 10Ω = <strong>2 V</strong>.<br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759066850/05f2900a-a2f0-4a6f-8653-7c2429459057.png\' alt=\'Circuit diagram for Q14a(ii)\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div><br/><br/><strong>OR (b)(i):</strong> Potential difference is 1 volt when 1 joule of work is done to move 1 coulomb of charge.<br/><strong>(b)(ii):</strong> Length l = <strong>68.75 m</strong>. If diameter is doubled, area becomes 4 times, so resistance becomes <strong>one-fourth</strong> (R becomes <sup>14</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = 3.5Ω).', hint: 'For (b)(ii), use R=ρ(l/A) and remember A=π(d/2)². R is inversely proportional to d².'},
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-15', question: `Q15: In the given circuit the total resistance between X and Y is:<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036474/1b4c6be6-0e84-46f3-83dc-037a238d4ebf.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q15' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, options: ['(a) 12Ω', '(b) 4 Ω', '(c) 6 Ω', '(d) 1 Ω'], answer: '(d) 1 Ω', hint: `This is a tricky circuit. The plain wires create short circuits. A simpler interpretation is that the three resistors (2Ω, 3Ω, 6Ω) are all connected in parallel. In that case: <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = <sup>(3+2+1)</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = 1. So, R = 1Ω.`, explanation: 'By observing the connections, we can see that one end of all three resistors (2Ω, 3Ω, and 6Ω) is connected to point X, and the other end of all three resistors is connected to point Y. This is the definition of a parallel connection. To find the equivalent resistance (R_eq), we use the formula for parallel resistors: <br/>1/R_eq = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 <br/>1/R_eq = (3+2+1)/6 = 6/6 = 1 <br/>Therefore, R_eq = 1 Ω.'},
    { section: '2024', id: '2024-16', type: 'long', question: 'Q16: Draw a circuit with a battery of four 1.5V cells, and 2Ω, 6Ω, 16Ω resistors in series. Put an ammeter and a voltmeter across the 16Ω resistor. Determine: (A) ammeter reading, and (B) voltmeter reading.', answer: '<div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759066957/47ea0891-2f60-461c-a70d-e312fb59dba6.png\' alt=\'Circuit diagram for Q16\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div><br/><br/>Total Voltage V = 4 × 1.5V = 6V.<br/>Total Resistance R = 2Ω + 6Ω + 16Ω = 24Ω.<br/><strong>(A) Ammeter reading (Total Current):</strong> I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>24Ω</sub> = <strong>0.25 A</strong>.<br/><strong>(B) Voltmeter reading (across 16Ω):</strong> V₁₆ = I × R₁₆ = 0.25A × 16Ω = <strong>4 V</strong>.', hint: 'Draw the circuit first. Then calculate total resistance and total current.'},
            
            // 2023 Questions
            { section: '2023', id: '2023-1', question: 'Q1: The expressions that relate (i) Q, I and t and (ii) Q, V and W respectively are:', options: [
                "(a) (i) I = Q/t (ii) W = V/Q",
                "(b) (i) Q = I × t (ii) W = V × Q",
                "(c) (i) Q = I/t (ii) V = W/Q",
                "(d) (i) I = Q/t (ii) Q = V/W",
              ], answer: '(b) (i) Q = I × t (ii) W = V × Q', hint: 'Recall the definitions of current (charge per time) and potential difference (work per charge).', explanation: '(i) Electric current (I) is defined as the rate of flow of charge (Q) per unit time (t). So, I = Q/t, which can be rearranged to Q = I × t. <br/>(ii) Electric potential difference (V) is defined as the work done (W) per unit charge (Q). So, V = W/Q, which can be rearranged to W = V × Q.' },
            { section: '2023', id: '2023-2', type: 'long', question: `Q2: <br/>(i) How is current related to potential difference? Draw a circuit to verify. <br/>(ii) Why should an ammeter have low resistance? <br/>(iii) For the two V-I graphs A and B for series and parallel combinations, which graph shows which combination?<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036933/44418017-039c-4714-93f5-0992a400c815.png' alt='I-V graph for Q2' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(i) Relationship & Circuit:</strong> Current (I) is directly proportional to potential difference (V) as per Ohm\'s Law. <div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759067097/263c6009-4704-4b4e-84f1-0c9a9f09c66a.png\' alt=\'Ohm\\\'s law verification circuit\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div> <br/><strong>(ii) Ammeter Resistance:</strong> An ammeter is connected in series and must have very low resistance so that it does not significantly change the total resistance and current of the circuit it is measuring. <br/><strong>(iii) Graphs:</strong> <div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759067215/818afa20-ce9b-4ba1-902a-cf949692f315.png\' alt=\'I-V graph analysis\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div> Graph A: The graph with a steeper slope corresponds to the series combination of resistors. <br/>Graph B: The graph with a flatter slope corresponds to the parallel combination of resistors.', hint: 'For (iii), remember that R_series > R_parallel.'},
            { section: '2023', id: '2023-3', type: 'long', question: 'Q3: <br/>(a) Define electric power and its SI unit. Relate the commercial unit (\'unit\') and joule. <br/>(b) 2 bulbs (50W each, 6h daily) and a geyser (1kW, 1h daily) are used. Calculate the total energy cost for a 30-day month at ₹8.00 per unit.', answer: '<strong>(a) Power & Units:</strong> <strong>Electric Power</strong> is the rate of electrical energy consumption. Its SI unit is the <strong>Watt (W)</strong>. The commercial unit is the kilowatt-hour (kWh). 1 unit = 1 kWh = <strong>3.6 × 10⁶ Joules</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Cost Calculation:</strong><br/>- Energy of bulbs per day = 2 × 50W × 6h = 600 Wh = 0.6 kWh.<br/>- Energy of geyser per day = 1kW × 1h = 1 kWh.<br/>- Total energy per day = 0.6 + 1 = 1.6 kWh.<br/>- Total energy in 30 days = 1.6 kWh/day × 30 days = 48 kWh (48 units).<br/>- Total cost = 48 units × ₹8.00/unit = <strong>₹384</strong>.', hint: 'Calculate total energy in kWh, then multiply by the rate.'},
            { section: '2023', id: '2023-4', question: 'Q4: If four identical resistors of 8 Ω are first connected in series (Rₛ) and then in parallel (Rₚ), then the ratio Rₛ / Rₚ is:', options: ['(a) 32', '(b) 2', '(c) 0.5', '(d) 16'], answer: '(d) 16', hint: 'Rₛ = nR = 4×8 = 32Ω. Rₚ = <sup>R</sup>&frasl;<sub>n</sub> = <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = 2Ω. Ratio = <sup>32</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = 16.', explanation: 'For \'n\' identical resistors of resistance R: <br/>- The series equivalent resistance is Rₛ = nR. Here, Rₛ = 4 × 8 Ω = 32 Ω. <br/>- The parallel equivalent resistance is Rₚ = R/n. Here, Rₚ = 8 Ω / 4 = 2 Ω. <br/>- The ratio Rₛ / Rₚ = 32 Ω / 2 Ω = 16.' },
            { section: '2023', id: '2023-5', question: `Q5: In the following diagram, the position of the needle is shown on the scale of a voltmeter. The least count and the reading shown by it respectively are:<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759036942/80be04f6-9d12-476a-8629-6baab78e6521.png' alt='Voltmeter for Q5' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, options: ['(a) 0.15 V and 1.6 V', '(b) 0.05 V and 1.6 V', '(c) 0.15 V and 1.8 V', '(d) 0.05 V and 1.8 V'], answer: '(c) 0.15 V and 1.8 V', hint: 'There are 10 divisions between 0 and 1.5V, so least count is <sup>1.5</sup>&frasl;<sub>10</sub> = 0.15V. The needle is at the 12th division, so reading is 12 × 0.15V = 1.8V.', explanation: 'First, find the least count (the value of one small division). There are 10 divisions between 0 V and 1.5 V. So, Least Count = 1.5 V / 10 divisions = 0.15 V/division. <br/>The needle is pointing to the 12th division from the start (0). <br/>Reading = Number of divisions × Least Count = 12 × 0.15 V = 1.8 V.' },
            { section: '2023', id: '2023-6', type: 'long', question: 'Q6: <br/>(A) An electric iron consumes 880 W at max rate and 330 W at min rate. If source is 220 V, calculate current and resistance in each case. <br/>(B) What is heating effect of electric current? <br/>(C) Find an expression for the amount of heat produced.', answer: '<strong>(A) Iron Calculation:</strong><br/>- <strong>Max Rate:</strong> I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>880</sup>&frasl;<sub>220</sub> = <strong>4 A</strong>. R = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub> = <sup>220</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = <strong>55 Ω</strong>.<br/>- <strong>Min Rate:</strong> I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>330</sup>&frasl;<sub>220</sub> = <strong>1.5 A</strong>. R = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub> = <sup>220</sup>&frasl;<sub>1.5</sub> ≈ <strong>146.7 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(B) Heating Effect:</strong> The phenomenon where the passage of electric current through a resistor produces heat.<br/><br/><strong>(C) Expression for Heat:</strong> Based on Joule\'s Law, the heat (H) produced is given by <strong>H = I²Rt</strong>.', hint: 'Use P=VI and R=V/I for part A.'},

            // Questions from 2022
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-1', type: 'long', question: 'Q1: <br/>(a) State Ohm\'s Law. Represent it mathematically. <br/>(b) Define 1 ohm. <br/>(c) What is the resistance of a conductor through which a current of 0.5 A flows when a potential difference of 2V is applied across its ends?', answer: '<strong>(a) Ohm\'s Law:</strong> At a constant temperature, the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across its ends. Mathematically, <strong>V = IR</strong>. <br/><br/><strong>(b) 1 Ohm:</strong> The resistance of a conductor is 1 ohm if a current of 1 ampere flows through it when a potential difference of 1 volt is applied. <br/><br/><strong>(c) Resistance Calculation:</strong> R = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub> = <sup>2 V</sup>&frasl;<sub>0.5 A</sub> = <strong>4 Ω</strong>.', hint: 'Ohm\'s law is the fundamental relationship between V, I, and R.' },
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-2', type: 'long', question: `Q2: In the figure, three cylindrical conductors A, B, C of the same material are shown with lengths and areas. Find (I) Rₐ/Rₑ, and (II)Rₐ/R𝒸.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037232/27d5bb84-d848-4166-aa41-b3efb938131b.png' alt='Conductors A, B, C for Q2' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: 'Let R = ρ(<sup>L</sup>&frasl;<sub>A</sub>).<br/>Rₐ = ρ(<sup>L</sup>&frasl;<sub>A</sub>)<br/>Rₑ = ρ(<sup>(L/2)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(2A)</sub>) = ρ(<sup>L</sup>&frasl;<sub>4A</sub>) = <sup>Rₐ</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub><br/>R𝒸 = ρ(<sup>(L/2)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(A/2)</sub>) = ρ(<sup>L</sup>&frasl;<sub>A</sub>) = Rₐ<br/><br/><strong>(I) <sup>Rₐ</sup>&frasl;<sub>Rₑ</sub> = <sup>Rₐ</sup>&frasl;<sub>(Rₐ/4)</sub> = 4.</strong><br/><strong>(II) <sup>Rₐ</sup>&frasl;<sub>R𝒸</sub> = <sup>Rₐ</sup>&frasl;<sub>Rₐ</sub> = 1.</strong>', hint: 'Write the resistance formula for each conductor and then find the ratios.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-3', type: 'long', question: 'Q3: <br/>(a)List the factors on which the resistance of a uniform cylindrical conductor depends. <br/>(b)The resistance of a wire of 0.01 cm radius is 10Ω. If the resistivity of the wire is 50 × 10⁻⁸ Ω m, find the length of this wire.', answer: '<strong>(a) Factors:</strong> Resistance depends on: 1. Length of the conductor (l), 2. Area of cross-section (A), 3. Nature of the material (resistivity ρ), and 4. Temperature.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Length Calculation:</strong> The length is <strong>0.628 m</strong>.<br/>r = 0.01cm = 10⁻⁴ m. A = πr² ≈ 3.14 × 10⁻⁸ m².<br/>l = <sup>(R×A)</sup>&frasl;<sub>ρ</sub> = <sup>(10 × 3.14 × 10⁻⁸)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(50 × 10⁻⁸)</sub> = <sup>31.4</sup>&frasl;<sub>50</sub> ≈ 0.628 m.', hint: 'Use the formula R=ρ(l/A) and be careful with units.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-4', type: 'brief', question: `Q4: Calculate the equivalent resistance of the following electric circuit:<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037258/5c599221-adc5-4aee-93e5-0034ed045f01.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q4' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: 'The equivalent resistance is <strong>40 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/>The middle 10Ω and 10Ω resistors are in series, giving 20Ω. This is in parallel with the top 20Ω resistor, giving an equivalent of <sup>(20×20)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(20+20)</sub> = 10Ω. This 10Ω is in series with the outer 10Ω and 20Ω resistors. Total R = 10Ω + 10Ω + 20Ω = 40Ω.', hint: 'Break down the circuit into series and parallel sections.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-5', type: 'long', question: `Q5: <br/>(i) Write formulas for equivalent resistance of series (I) and parallel (II) combinations. <br/>(ii) Which V-I graph is correctly labelled for series (Rₛ) and parallel (Rₚ) combinations? Justify.<br/><br/><div class='flex justify-center items-center flex-wrap gap-4 my-4'><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037264/1bb4177d-eff8-4bfe-8f6a-94f7c06dc9dd.png' alt='Combinations for Q5i' class='rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image'/><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037268/23ff1377-779e-42d9-97ab-d471dc04c0e4.png' alt='V-I graphs for Q5ii' class='rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image'/></div>`, answer: '<strong>(i) Formulas:</strong> Series Rₛ = R₁ + R₂ + R₃. Parallel <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>Rₚ</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₁</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₂</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₃</sub>.<br/><br/><strong>(ii) Correct Graph:</strong> <strong>Graph (II) is correctly labelled.</strong><br/><strong>Justification:</strong> The slope of a V-I graph is Resistance (R=<sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub>). We know that series resistance (Rₛ) is always greater than parallel resistance (Rₚ). Therefore, the line with the steeper slope must be Rₛ, and the line with the less steep slope must be Rₚ. Graph (II) correctly shows this.', hint: 'Slope of V-I graph is resistance. R_series > R_parallel.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-6', type: 'long', question: `Q6: Study the circuit shown with arms A, B, C.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037271/95bac042-f594-4ef6-971e-cae651855209.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q6' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div><br/>(a) Find equivalent resistance of arm A.<br/>(b) Calculate equivalent resistance of parallel arms B and C.<br/>(c)(i) Determine current through ammeter <div class="text-center my-4"><strong>OR</strong></div> (ii) Determine current if arm B is withdrawn.`, answer: '<strong>(a) Resistance of A:</strong> Rₐ = 5 + 15 + 20 = <strong>40 Ω</strong> (Series).<br/><br/><strong>(b) Resistance of B & C:</strong> Rₑ = 5 + 10 + 15 = 30 Ω. R𝒸 = 10 + 20 + 30 = 60 Ω. They are in parallel. R_BC = <sup>(30×60)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(30+60)</sub> = <sup>1800</sup>&frasl;<sub>90</sub> = <strong>20 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(c)(i) Ammeter Current:</strong> Total R = Rₐ + R_BC = 40 + 20 = 60 Ω. Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>60Ω</sub> = <strong>0.1 A</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>OR (ii) Current without arm B:</strong> The circuit becomes arm A in series with arm C. Total R = Rₐ + R𝒸 = 40 + 60 = 100 Ω. Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>100Ω</sub> = <strong>0.06 A</strong>.', hint: 'Calculate resistance for each arm first, then combine them.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-7', type: 'long', question: 'Q7: <br/>(i) State Joule\'s law of heating and express it mathematically. <br/>(ii) A 5 Ω resistor is connected to a 6V battery. Calculate heat dissipated in 10s.', answer: '<strong>(i) Joule\'s Law of Heating:</strong> It states that the heat produced in a resistor is directly proportional to the square of the current, the resistance, and the time for which the current flows. Mathematically, <strong>H = I²Rt</strong>. <br/><br/><strong>(ii) Calculation:</strong> Using H = (<sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>)t, <br/> H = (<sup>6²</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub>) × 10 = (<sup>36</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub>) × 10 = 36 × 2 = <strong>72 J</strong>.', hint: 'Use the form of Joule\'s law that best fits the given variables (V, R, t).' },
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-8', type: 'long', question: 'Q8: <br/>(a) Calculate resistance of a metal wire of length 2 m, area 1.55 × 10⁻⁶ m², and resistivity 2.8 × 10⁻⁸ Ωm. <br/>(b) Why are alloys preferred over pure metals for heating elements?', answer: '<strong>(a) Resistance:</strong> R = ρ(<sup>l</sup>&frasl;<sub>A</sub>) = <sup>(2.8 × 10⁻⁸ × 2)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(1.55 × 10⁻⁶)</sub> ≈ <strong>0.036 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Alloys Preference:</strong> They are preferred because they have <strong>higher resistivity</strong> (produce more heat) and a <strong>high melting point</strong>, and they do not oxidize easily.', hint: 'Plug the values directly into the resistivity formula.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-9', type: 'brief', question: 'Q9: An electric heater rated 1100 W operates at 220 V. Calculate (i) its resistance, and (ii) the current drawn.', answer: '<strong>(i) Resistance:</strong> R = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>P</sub> = <sup>(220)²</sup>&frasl;<sub>1100</sub> = <sup>48400</sup>&frasl;<sub>1100</sub> = <strong>44 Ω</strong>.<br/><strong>(ii) Current:</strong> I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>1100W</sup>&frasl;<sub>220V</sub> = <strong>5 A</strong>.', hint: 'Use the power formulas P=V²/R and P=VI.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-10', type: 'long', question: 'Q10: <br/>(a) What is the meaning of electric power? Write its SI unit. <br/>(b) An electric kettle of 2kW is used for 2 hours. Calculate energy consumed in (i) kWh and (ii) joules.', answer: '<strong>(a) Electric Power:</strong> The rate at which electrical energy is consumed. Its SI unit is the <strong>Watt (W)</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Energy Calculation:</strong><br/>(i) Energy (kWh) = Power (kW) × Time (h) = 2 kW × 2 h = <strong>4 kWh</strong>.<br/>(ii) Energy (J) = 4 kWh × 3.6 × 10⁶ J/kWh = <strong>14.4 × 10⁶ J</strong>.', hint: '1 kWh = 3.6 million Joules.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-11', type: 'long', question: 'Q11: <br/>(i) Define electric power and write its SI unit. <br/>(ii) Two lamps, one rated 100 W at 220 V, and the other 60 W at 220 V, are connected in parallel to mains. What current is drawn from the line if the supply voltage is 220 V?', answer: '<strong>(i) Electric Power:</strong> The rate of consumption of electrical energy. SI unit is <strong>Watt (W)</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(ii) Current Drawn:</strong> Total current is <strong>0.73 A</strong> (approx).<br/>Total Power P = 100W + 60W = 160W.<br/>Total Current I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>160W</sup>&frasl;<sub>220V</sub> ≈ 0.727 A.', hint: 'In parallel, total power is the sum of individual powers.'},
            { section: '2022', id: '2022-12', type: 'long', question: 'Q12: Define electric power. Derive an expression for power in terms of V and R. What is the power of a device of resistance 400 Ω operating at 200 V?', answer: '<strong>Electric Power:</strong> The rate at which electrical energy is consumed.<br/><strong>Derivation:</strong> Power P = <sup>W</sup>&frasl;<sub>t</sub>. We know W = VQ and Q = It. So P = <sup>(VIt)</sup>&frasl;<sub>t</sub> = VI. From Ohm\'s law, I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>. Substituting, we get P = V(<sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>) = <strong><sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub></strong>.<br/><br/><strong>Power Calculation:</strong> P = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>(200)²</sup>&frasl;<sub>400</sub> = <sup>40000</sup>&frasl;<sub>400</sub> = <strong>100 W</strong>.', hint: 'Start from the basic definition of power, P=W/t.'},
            
            // 2021 Questions
            { section: '2021', id: '2021-1', type: 'brief', question: 'Q1: Two lamps one rated 100W at 220V, and the other 60W at 220V, are connected in parallel to electric mains supply. What current is drawn from the line if the supply voltage is 220V?', answer: 'The total current drawn is <strong>0.73 A</strong> (approx).<br/><br/>The total power consumed in parallel is P = P₁ + P₂ = 100 W + 60 W = 160 W.<br/>The total current I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>160 W</sup>&frasl;<sub>220 V</sub> ≈ 0.727 A.', hint: 'In parallel, you can add the powers of the appliances to find the total power.' },
            
            // 2020 Questions
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-1', question: 'Q1: If a person has five resistors each of value 5Ω, then the maximum resistance he can obtain by connecting them is:', options: ['(a) 1Ω', '(b) 5Ω', '(c) 10Ω', '(d) 25Ω'], answer: '(d) 25Ω', hint: 'Maximum resistance is obtained by connecting all resistors in series. R_max = 5 × 5Ω = 25Ω.', explanation: 'To obtain the maximum possible resistance from a set of resistors, they must be connected in series. The total resistance in a series circuit is the sum of the individual resistances. <br/>R_max = 5Ω + 5Ω + 5Ω + 5Ω + 5Ω = 5 × 5Ω = 25Ω.' },
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-2', question: 'Q2: The maximum resistance which can be made using four resistors each of resistance 2 Ω is:', options: ['(a) 2 Ω', '(b) 1 Ω', '(c) 2.5 Ω', '(d) 8 Ω'], answer: '(d) 8 Ω', hint: 'Maximum resistance is achieved by connecting in series: 4 × 2Ω = 8Ω.', explanation: 'Maximum resistance is achieved by connecting all resistors in series. The total resistance is the sum of the individual resistances: <br/>R_max = 2Ω + 2Ω + 2Ω + 2Ω = 4 × 2Ω = 8Ω.' },
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-3', question: 'Q3: The maximum resistance which can be made using four resistors each of resistance 1/2 Ω is:', options: ['(a) 2Ω', '(b) 1Ω', '(c) 2.5Ω', '(d) 8Ω'], answer: '(a) 2Ω', hint: 'Connect in series: 4 × (<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>)Ω = 2Ω.', explanation: 'Maximum resistance is achieved by connecting all resistors in series. The total resistance is the sum of the individual resistances: <br/>R_max = 0.5Ω + 0.5Ω + 0.5Ω + 0.5Ω = 4 × 0.5Ω = 2Ω.' },
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-4', type: 'long', question: `Q4: A V-I graph for a nichrome wire is given. What do you infer? Draw a labelled circuit diagram to obtain such a graph.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037650/d94b6c7a-27dc-4d80-907b-ac702209c3ae.png' alt='V-I graph for Q4' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>Inference:</strong> The straight line graph passing through the origin shows that the potential difference (V) is directly proportional to the current (I). This verifies Ohm\'s Law. The resistance of the nichrome wire is constant.<br/><br/><strong>Circuit Diagram:</strong><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759067673/6bc4ac43-c60f-4de2-a149-82e258f8bbf7.png\' alt=\'Ohm\\\'s law verification circuit\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div>', hint: 'A straight V-I graph through the origin indicates a constant resistance.'},
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-5', type: 'long', question: 'Q5: <br/>(a) Write the mathematical expression for Joule\'s law of heating. <br/>(b) Compute the heat generated while transferring 96000 coulomb of charge in two hours through a potential difference of 40 V.', answer: '<strong>(a) Joule\'s Law:</strong> H = I²Rt.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Heat Calculation:</strong> The heat generated is <strong>3,840,000 J</strong> or <strong>3.84 × 10⁶ J</strong>.<br/>We can use the formula H = W = V × Q.<br/>H = 40 V × 96000 C = 3,840,000 J.', hint: 'The work done in moving a charge is equal to the heat generated. Use H = VQ.'},
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-6', question: 'Q6: Two bulbs of 100 W and 40 W are connected in series. The current through the 100 W bulb is 1A. The current through the 40 W bulb will be:', options: ['(a) 0.4A', '(b) 0.6A', '(c) 0.8A', '(d) 1A'], answer: '(d) 1A', hint: 'In a series circuit, the current is the same through all components.', explanation: 'In any series circuit, the electric current is the same at every point. The current flows through each component sequentially without splitting. Therefore, if the current flowing through the 100 W bulb is 1A, the current flowing through the 40 W bulb must also be 1A.' },
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-7', type: 'long', question: 'Q7: <br/>(a) What is meant by "The resistance of a conductor is one ohm"? <br/>(b) Define electric power and write an expression relating P, V and R. <br/>(c) How many 132 Ω resistors in parallel are required to carry 5 A on a 220 V line?', answer: '<strong>(a) One Ohm:</strong> Resistance is 1Ω when a potential difference of 1V causes a current of 1A to flow.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Electric Power:</strong> The rate of consumption of electrical energy. P = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>.<br/><br/><strong>(c) Number of resistors:</strong> <strong>3 resistors</strong> are required.<br/>The total resistance required is R_total = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub> = <sup>220V</sup>&frasl;<sub>5A</sub> = 44Ω. For \'n\' parallel resistors, R_total = <sup>R</sup>&frasl;<sub>n</sub>. So, 44 = <sup>132</sup>&frasl;<sub>n</sub>, which gives n = <sup>132</sup>&frasl;<sub>44</sub> = 3.', hint: 'First find the total required resistance of the circuit.'},
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-8', type: 'long', question: 'Q8: <br/>(a) Write the mathematical expression for Joule\'s law of heating. <br/>(b) Compute the heat generated while transferring 96000 coulombs of charge in two hours through a potential difference of 40 V.', answer: '<strong>(a) Joule\'s Law:</strong> H = I²Rt.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Heat Calculation:</strong> The heat generated is <strong>3,840,000 J</strong> or <strong>3.84 × 10⁶ J</strong>.<br/>We can use the formula H = W = V × Q.<br/>H = 40 V × 96000 C = 3,840,000 J.', hint: 'This is a repeat of Q5.'},
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-9', question: 'Q9: A conductor of length ‘l‘, area ‘A‘ has resistance ‘R‘. Another conductor of length 2.5l, resistance 0.5R and of the same material has area of cross-section:', options: ['(a) 5A', '(b) 2.5A', '(c) 0.5A', '(d) 1 / 5A'], answer: '(a) 5A', hint: 'Use the ratio <sup>R₁</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₂</sub> = <sup>(l₁/A₁)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(l₂/A₂)</sub>. Since ρ is the same, it cancels out.', explanation: 'Resistance R = ρ(l/A). For the first wire: R₁ = R, l₁ = l, A₁ = A. For the second wire: R₂ = 0.5R, l₂ = 2.5l. We need A₂. <br/>Since ρ is the same, we can write the ratio: R₁/R₂ = (l₁/A₁) / (l₂/A₂) = (l₁A₂)/(l₂A₁). <br/>Substituting the values: R/(0.5R) = (l × A₂)/(2.5l × A). <br/>2 = A₂/(2.5A). <br/>A₂ = 2 × 2.5A = 5A.'},
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-10', question: 'Q10: The resistance of a resistor is reduced to half of its initial value. If other parameters remain unchanged, the heating effects will become:', options: ['(a) two times', '(b) half', '(c) one-fourth', '(d) four times'], answer: '(a) two times', hint: 'Heating effect (Power) P = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>. If voltage is constant, power is inversely proportional to resistance.', explanation: 'The heating effect is equivalent to the power dissipated (P). The formula relating power, voltage (V), and resistance (R) is P = V²/R. The problem states that other parameters (like voltage) are unchanged, so power is inversely proportional to resistance (P ∝ 1/R). If the resistance is halved (R → R/2), the new power P\' = V²/(R/2) = 2(V²/R) = 2P. The heating effect doubles.' },
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-11', question: 'Q11: Assertion (A): Alloys are commonly used in electrical heating devices. <br/>Reason (R): Resistivity of an alloy is generally higher than that of its constituent metals but the alloys have low melting points.', options: ['(a) Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is correct explanation.', '(b) Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not correct explanation.', '(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.', '(d) (A) is false but (R) is true.'], answer: '(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.', hint: 'Alloys used for heating elements must have high melting points.', explanation: 'Assertion (A) is true. Alloys like nichrome are used for heating elements. Reason (R) is partially true but contains a critical error. While the resistivity of an alloy is indeed higher than its constituent metals, alloys used for heating elements must have HIGH melting points to operate without melting. The reason incorrectly states they have low melting points, making the reason false.'},
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-12', question: 'Q12: Assertion (A): At high temperatures, metal wires have a greater chance of short circuiting.<br/>Reason (R): Both resistance and resistivity of a material vary with temperature.', options: ['(a) Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is correct explanation.', '(b) Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not correct explanation.', '(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.', '(d) (A) is false but (R) is true.'], answer: '(b) Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).', hint: 'While both statements are true, the reason for short-circuiting is typically insulation failure due to heat, not just the change in resistance itself.', explanation: 'Assertion (A) is a practical truth; excessive heat can damage the plastic insulation around wires, causing the live and neutral wires to touch, leading to a short circuit. Reason (R) is also a true physical principle; the resistance and resistivity of metals increase with temperature. However, the change in resistance is not the direct cause of the short circuit. The insulation failure is. Therefore, R is not the correct explanation for A.'},
            { section: '2020', id: '2020-13', type: 'long', question: 'Q13: <br/>(A) An electric bulb is rated at 200 V; 100 W. What is its resistance? <br/>(B) Calculate energy consumed by 3 such bulbs for 10 hours for the month of November. <br/>(C) Calculate total cost if the rate is ₹ 6.50 per unit.', answer: '<strong>(A) Resistance:</strong> R = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>P</sub> = <sup>(200)²</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub> = <sup>40000</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub> = <strong>400 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(B) Energy Consumed:</strong> Power of 3 bulbs = 3 × 100W = 300W = 0.3 kW. November has 30 days. Total hours = 10h/day × 30 days = 300h. Total Energy = Power × Time = 0.3kW × 300h = <strong>90 kWh</strong> (90 units).<br/><br/><strong>(C) Total Cost:</strong> Cost = 90 units × ₹6.50/unit = <strong>₹585</strong>.', hint: 'Calculate total power and total time first to find the energy in kWh.'},

            // 2019 Questions
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-1', question: `Q1: A student plots the V-I graph of three samples of nichrome wire with resistances R₁, R₂, and R₃. Which is true?<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037774/c595e1f9-7073-4a82-a7fb-bcc28f421687.png' alt='I-V graph for Q1' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: `(c) R₃ > R₂ > R₁`, hint: 'Check the axes carefully! Slope of a V-I graph is R. Slope of an I-V graph is 1/R.', explanation: 'The graph shows Current (I) on the y-axis and Voltage (V) on the x-axis. The slope of this I-V graph is I/V. According to Ohm\'s Law, R = V/I, so the slope is equal to 1/R. This means that a steeper slope corresponds to a smaller resistance. Since the slope of line for R₁ is the steepest and the slope for R₃ is the least steep, their resistances are in the order R₁ < R₂ < R₃.' },
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-2', type: 'brief', question: 'Q2: When do we say that the potential difference between two points of a circuit in 1 volt?', answer: 'The potential difference is <strong>1 volt</strong> if <strong>1 joule</strong> of work is done to move a charge of <strong>1 coulomb</strong> from one point to the other.', hint: 'It relates work, charge, and potential difference.' },
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-3', type: 'brief', question: 'Q3: Define resistance. Give its SI unit.', answer: '<strong>Resistance</strong> is the property of a conductor to oppose the flow of electric current. Its SI unit is the <strong>Ohm (Ω)</strong>.', hint: 'It is the opposition to current flow.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-4', type: 'brief', question: 'Q4: State Ohm’s law.', answer: '<strong>Ohm\'s Law</strong> states that at a constant temperature, the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across its ends (V ∝ I).', hint: 'It describes the relationship between V and I.' },
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-5', type: 'long', question: `Q5: In the circuit given, R₁=5Ω, R₂=10Ω, R₃=30Ω are connected in parallel to a 12V battery. Calculate <br/>(a) current through each resistor, <br/>(b) total circuit resistance, <br/>(c) total current.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037791/3256d3f5-a5c2-47e9-83c0-ce7f557ad330.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q5' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(a) Current through each:</strong><br/>I₁ = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₁</sub> = <sup>12V</sup>&frasl;<sub>5Ω</sub> = <strong>2.4 A</strong><br/>I₂ = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₂</sub> = <sup>12V</sup>&frasl;<sub>10Ω</sub> = <strong>1.2 A</strong><br/>I₃ = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₃</sub> = <sup>12V</sup>&frasl;<sub>30Ω</sub> = <strong>0.4 A</strong><br/><br/><strong>(b) Total Resistance:</strong><br/><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_total</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>10</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>30</sub> = <sup>(6+3+1)</sup>&frasl;<sub>30</sub> = <sup>10</sup>&frasl;<sub>30</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>. So, R_total = <strong>3 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(c) Total Current:</strong><br/>I_total = I₁ + I₂ + I₃ = 2.4 + 1.2 + 0.4 = <strong>4 A</strong>. (Alternatively, I_total = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_total</sub> = <sup>12V</sup>&frasl;<sub>3Ω</sub> = 4 A).', hint: 'In parallel, voltage is the same across all resistors.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-6', type: 'brief', question: 'Q6: On what factors does the resistance of a conductor depend?', answer: 'Resistance of a conductor depends on: <br/>1. Its <strong>length (l)</strong>.<br/>2. Its <strong>area of cross-section (A)</strong>.<br/>3. The <strong>nature of its material (resistivity ρ)</strong>.<br/>4. Its <strong>temperature</strong>.<br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759067972/84ab1bc3-42b5-4599-b71e-161b1f7063b1.png\' alt=\'Factors affecting resistance\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div>', hint: 'Think of the formula R = ρ(l/A).'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-7', type: 'long', question: 'Q7: <br/>(a) A bulb is rated 40 W, 220 V. Find the current and resistance. <br/>(b) If replaced by a 25 W, 220 V bulb, what is the change in current and resistance?', answer: '<strong>(a) For 40W bulb:</strong><br/>Current I = <sup>P</sup>&frasl;<sub>V</sub> = <sup>40</sup>&frasl;<sub>220</sub> ≈ <strong>0.18 A</strong>.<br/>Resistance R = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>P</sub> = <sup>220²</sup>&frasl;<sub>40</sub> = <strong>1210 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) For 25W bulb and change:</strong><br/>New Current I\' = <sup>25</sup>&frasl;<sub>220</sub> ≈ <strong>0.11 A</strong>.<br/>New Resistance R\' = <sup>220²</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub> = <strong>1936 Ω</strong>.<br/><strong>Change:</strong> The current decreases and the resistance increases. A lower power bulb has higher resistance and draws less current.', hint: 'Lower power rating at the same voltage means higher resistance.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-8', type: 'long', question: 'Q8: Derive the expression for power P consumed by a device having resistance R and potential difference V.', answer: 'We know that Power (P) is the rate of doing work (<sup>W</sup>&frasl;<sub>t</sub>). So, P = <sup>W</sup>&frasl;<sub>t</sub>.<br/>Potential difference V = <sup>W</sup>&frasl;<sub>Q</sub>, so W = VQ.<br/>Substituting this, P = <sup>VQ</sup>&frasl;<sub>t</sub>.<br/>Current I = <sup>Q</sup>&frasl;<sub>t</sub>, so we get P = VI.<br/>From Ohm\'s Law, I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>. Substituting this into P=VI gives P = V(<sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>) = <strong><sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub></strong>.<br/>Alternatively, substituting V=IR gives P = (IR)I = <strong>I²R</strong>.', hint: 'Start from the basic definitions of power, voltage and current.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-9', type: 'long', question: `Q9: Compare the power used in 2 Ω resistor in each of the following circuits.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037794/7c700672-8d0e-4422-b386-23439750d056.png' alt='Circuit diagrams for Q9' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>Circuit (a) - Series:</strong><br/>Total R = 1Ω + 2Ω = 3Ω. Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>3Ω</sub> = 2A.<br/>Power in 2Ω resistor Pₐ = I²R = (2)² × 2 = <strong>8 W</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>Circuit (b) - Parallel:</strong><br/>The voltage across the parallel combination is 4V. The voltage across the 2Ω resistor is 4V.<br/>Power in 2Ω resistor Pₑ = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>(4)²</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = <sup>16</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = <strong>8 W</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>Comparison:</strong> The power consumed in the 2Ω resistor is the <strong>same in both circuits (8 W)</strong>.', hint: 'For series, find current first. For parallel, use voltage.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-10', type: 'long', question: 'Q10: Derive the relation R = R₁ + R₂ + R₃ when resistors are joined in series.', answer: 'When resistors are in series, the same current (I) flows through all of them. The total potential difference (V) across the combination is the sum of the potential differences across each resistor: V = V₁ + V₂ + V₃.<br/>Using Ohm\'s Law (V=IR) for each resistor: V₁=IR₁, V₂=IR₂, V₃=IR₃.<br/>For the whole circuit, V = IR_eq.<br/>Substituting these into the voltage equation: IR_eq = IR₁ + IR₂ + IR₃.<br/>Dividing by I on both sides gives: <strong>R_eq = R₁ + R₂ + R₃</strong>.', hint: 'Start with the fact that voltage adds up in a series circuit.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-11', type: 'long', question: 'Q11: <br/>(a) Write the relation between resistance and resistivity and derive the SI unit of resistivity. <br/>(b) Resistance of a 5m wire is 100Ω. If area is 3×10⁻⁷ m², calculate resistivity.', answer: '<strong>(a) Relation and Unit:</strong> The relation is <strong>R = ρ(<sup>l</sup>&frasl;<sub>A</sub>)</strong>. The SI unit of resistivity (ρ) is the <strong>Ohm-meter (Ω·m)</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Resistivity Calculation:</strong><br/>ρ = <sup>(R×A)</sup>&frasl;<sub>l</sub> = <sup>(100Ω × 3×10⁻⁷ m²)</sup>&frasl;<sub>5m</sub> = <strong>60 × 10⁻⁷ Ω·m</strong> or <strong>6 × 10⁻⁶ Ω·m</strong>.', hint: 'Rearrange the resistance formula to solve for ρ.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-12', type: 'long', question: 'Q12: <br/>(a) Derive the formula for equivalent resistance in parallel. <br/>(b) Two 12Ω resistors are joined in parallel to a 6V battery. Find the current drawn.', answer: '<strong>(a) Derivation for Parallel Resistance:</strong><br/>In a parallel circuit, the voltage (V) across each resistor is the same. The total current (I) is the sum of currents in each branch: I = I₁ + I₂ + I₃.<br/>Using Ohm\'s Law (I=<sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub>) for each branch: I₁=<sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₁</sub>, I₂=<sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₂</sub>, I₃=<sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₃</sub>.<br/>For the whole circuit, I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_eq</sub>.<br/>Substituting: <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_eq</sub> = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₁</sub> + <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₂</sub> + <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₃</sub>.<br/>Dividing by V gives: <strong><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_eq</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₁</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₂</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₃</sub></strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Current Calculation:</strong><br/>Equivalent resistance R_eq = <sup>(12×12)</sup>&frasl;<sub>(12+12)</sub> = <sup>144</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub> = 6Ω.<br/>Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_eq</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>6Ω</sub> = <strong>1 A</strong>.', hint: 'Start with the fact that current splits in a parallel circuit.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-13', type: 'long', question: `Q13: Study the circuit and find: <br/>(i) Current in 12 Ω resistor <br/>(ii) difference in readings of A₁ and A₂, if any.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037856/68e032c0-83c6-46b6-806a-3a750502e910.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q13' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: 'First, find total resistance. The two 24Ω resistors are in parallel, giving Rₚ = 12Ω. This is in series with the 12Ω resistor, so R_total = 12+12=24Ω. Total current (reading of A₂) is I_total = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_total</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>24Ω</sub> = 0.25 A.<br/>This 0.25A current flows through the 12Ω resistor. So, <strong>(i) Current in 12Ω resistor is 0.25 A</strong>.<br/><br/>The current splits equally between the two 24Ω paths. The current in the upper path (reading of A₁) is <sup>0.25A</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = 0.125 A.<br/><strong>(ii) Difference in readings:</strong> Reading A₂ = 0.25A, Reading A₁ = 0.125A. Difference = 0.25 - 0.125 = <strong>0.125 A</strong>.', hint: 'A₂ measures total current, while A₁ measures current in only one parallel branch.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-14', type: 'long', question: 'Q14: <br/>(i) Draw a circuit with five 2V cells, 5Ω, 10Ω, 15Ω resistors, and a key, all in series. <br/>(ii) Calculate current. <br/>(iii) Find potential difference across 15Ω resistor.', answer: '<strong>(i) Circuit Diagram:</strong><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759068050/e56f862e-7b7b-45f8-8283-a44b61c48537.png\' alt=\'Series circuit diagram for Q14\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div><br/><br/><strong>(ii) Current Calculation:</strong> Total V = 5 × 2V = 10V. Total R = 5+10+15 = 30Ω. Current I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>10V</sup>&frasl;<sub>30Ω</sub> = <strong><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> A ≈ 0.33 A</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(iii) Potential Difference:</strong> V₁₅ = I × R₁₅ = (<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> A) × 15Ω = <strong>5 V</strong>.', hint: 'Find total voltage and total resistance first.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-15', type: 'long', question: `Q15: An electric lamp (20Ω) and a conductor (4Ω) are connected in series to a 6V battery. Calculate <br/>(a) total resistance, <br/>(b) total current, <br/>(c) potential difference across each, <br/>(d) power of the lamp.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037870/c8ec5109-6833-496a-8e7d-1b545db16c4d.png' alt='Circuit diagram for Q15' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(a) Total Resistance:</strong> R_total = 20Ω + 4Ω = <strong>24 Ω</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Total Current:</strong> I = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>R</sub> = <sup>6V</sup>&frasl;<sub>24Ω</sub> = <strong>0.25 A</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(c) Potential Difference:</strong><br/>(i) Lamp: V_lamp = I × R_lamp = 0.25A × 20Ω = <strong>5 V</strong>.<br/>(ii) Conductor: V_cond = I × R_cond = 0.25A × 4Ω = <strong>1 V</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>(d) Power of Lamp:</strong> P_lamp = V_lamp × I = 5V × 0.25A = <strong>1.25 W</strong> (or P=I²R = 0.25² × 20 = 1.25W).', hint: 'This is a straightforward series circuit calculation.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-16', type: 'long', question: `Q16: <br/>(a) Draw a circuit for three parallel resistors and derive the expression for equivalent resistance. <br/>(b) Calculate the equivalent resistance of the network shown.<br/><br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759037874/259c20f7-acc7-428e-b0a8-2045b57c736d.png' alt='Resistor network for Q16b' class='mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image' /></div>`, answer: '<strong>(a) Circuit and Derivation:</strong> See Q12(a) for the derivation. <div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759068130/0d742af8-721e-4e8f-980d-67735ab52055.png\' alt=\'Parallel circuit for Q16\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div><br/><br/><strong>(b) Equivalent Resistance:</strong> The two 20Ω resistors are in parallel, Rₚ = <sup>20</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = 10Ω. This is in series with the 10Ω resistor. R_total = 10Ω + 10Ω = <strong>20 Ω</strong>.', hint: 'Solve the parallel part first.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-17', type: 'long', question: 'Q17: <br/>(a) Write expressions for equivalent resistance in series and parallel. <br/>(b) Two identical 12Ω resistors are connected to a 3V battery. Calculate the ratio of power consumed for minimum resistance vs maximum resistance.', answer: '<strong>(a) Expressions:</strong> Series: R_s = R₁+R₂+R₃. Parallel: <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_p</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₁</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₂</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>R₃</sub>.<br/><br/><strong>(b) Power Ratio:</strong> The ratio is <strong>4:1</strong>.<br/>- Minimum Resistance (Parallel): R_min = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = 6Ω. Power P_min = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_min</sub> = <sup>3²</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = 1.5W.<br/>- Maximum Resistance (Series): R_max = 12+12 = 24Ω. Power P_max = <sup>V²</sup>&frasl;<sub>R_max</sub> = <sup>3²</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub> = <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub> = 0.375W.<br/>- Ratio <sup>P_min</sup>&frasl;<sub>P_max</sub> = <sup>1.5</sup>&frasl;<sub>0.375</sub> = 4. So the ratio is 4:1.', hint: 'Minimum resistance is parallel, maximum is series.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-18', type: 'long', question: 'Q18: Experimentally prove that in series combination: <br/>(a) current is same, and <br/>(b) total potential difference is sum of individual potential differences.', answer: '<strong>Experimental Setup:</strong> Connect three resistors (R₁, R₂, R₃) in series with a battery, key, and ammeter. Connect voltmeters (V₁, V₂, V₃) in parallel across each resistor and a total voltmeter (V) across the combination.<br/><br/><strong>Procedure & Observation:</strong><br/>1. Close the key. Note the ammeter reading (I).<br/>2. Move the ammeter to a position between R₁ and R₂, then between R₂ and R₃. Observe that the ammeter reading <strong>I remains the same</strong>. This proves (a).<br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759068477/2f6fbc4c-8156-4e62-b697-daadbc31e19a.png\' alt=\'Series circuit current experiment\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div><br/>3. Note the readings of V₁, V₂, V₃, and the total voltage V. Observe that <strong>V = V₁ + V₂ + V₃</strong>. This proves (b).<br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759068474/563ca673-5959-4b5b-af4a-4a0c1ffd5159.png\' alt=\'Series circuit voltage experiment\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div>', hint: 'Describe the placement of ammeters and voltmeters and what you would observe.'},
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-19', question: 'Q19: Unit of electric power may also be expressed as:', options: ['(a) volt-ampere', '(b) kilowatt-hour', '(c) watt-second', '(d) joule-second'], answer: '(a) volt-ampere<br/><div class="w-full overflow-x-auto"><img src=\'https://res.cloudinary.com/dxwszplz7/image/upload/v1759067817/73969a35-d8d3-42c8-b64d-39d5801a0ded.png\' alt=\'Power formula triangle\' class=\'mx-auto my-4 rounded-lg shadow-md theme-image\' /></div>', hint: 'Consider the formula P = V × I.', explanation: 'Electric power (P) is the product of potential difference (V) and current (I), given by the formula P = V × I. The SI unit for potential difference is the Volt (V) and for current is the Ampere (A). Therefore, the unit for power can also be expressed as Volt-Ampere.' },
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-20', question: 'Q20: When a 4 V battery is connected across an unknown resistor there is a current of 100 mA. The value of the resistance is:', options: ['(a) 4 Ω', '(b) 40 Ω', '(c) 400 Ω', '(d) 0.4 Ω'], answer: '(b) 40 Ω', hint: 'Use Ohm\'s Law, R = <sup>V</sup>&frasl;<sub>I</sub>. Remember to convert mA to A (100mA = 0.1A).', explanation: 'According to Ohm\'s Law, Resistance (R) = Voltage (V) / Current (I). <br/>First, convert the current from milliamperes (mA) to amperes (A): 100 mA = 100 / 1000 A = 0.1 A. <br/>Then, calculate the resistance: R = 4 V / 0.1 A = 40 Ω.' },
            { section: '2019', id: '2019-21', type: 'brief', question: 'Q21: <br/>(A) An ammeter has 10 divisions between 0 and 0.5 A. If the needle indicates the 12th division, what is the reading? <br/>(B) How do you connect an ammeter and a voltmeter in a circuit?', answer: '<strong>(A) Ammeter Reading:</strong> The reading is <strong>0.6 A</strong>. <br/>Least Count = <sup>0.5A</sup>&frasl;<sub>10 divisions</sub> = 0.05 A/division. <br/>Reading = 12 divisions × 0.05 A/division = 0.6 A.<br/><br/><strong>(B) Connections:</strong> An <strong>ammeter</strong> is always connected in <strong>series</strong>. A <strong>voltmeter</strong> is always connected in <strong>parallel</strong>.', hint: 'First find the value of one division (least count).'}
        ];


        // --- TIMER AND THEME COMPONENT ---
        const TimerAndTheme = ({ setCurrentTheme }) => {
            const [time, setTime] = useState(0);
            const [isActive, setIsActive] = useState(false);
            const [isThemeOpen, setIsThemeOpen] = useState(false);
            const themeRef = useRef(null);

            useEffect(() => {
                let interval = null;
                if (isActive) {
                    interval = setInterval(() => {
                        setTime((time) => time + 1);
                    }, 1000);
                } else if (!isActive && time !== 0) {
                    clearInterval(interval);
                }
                return () => clearInterval(interval);
            }, [isActive, time]);

            useEffect(() => {
                const handleClickOutside = (event) => {
                    if (themeRef.current && !themeRef.current.contains(event.target)) {
                        setIsThemeOpen(false);
                    }
                };
                document.addEventListener("mousedown", handleClickOutside);
                return () => {
                    document.removeEventListener("mousedown", handleClickOutside);
                };
            }, [themeRef]);

            const formatTime = () => {
                const hours = Math.floor(time / 3600);
                const minutes = Math.floor((time % 3600) / 60);
                const seconds = time % 60;
                return `${hours.toString().padStart(2, '0')}:${minutes.toString().padStart(2, '0')}:${seconds.toString().padStart(2, '0')}`;
            };

            return (
                 <div className="relative flex items-center gap-2 p-2 rounded-full bg-[var(--theme-card-bg)] shadow-md" ref={themeRef}>
                    <TimerIcon />
                    <span className="font-mono text-lg">{formatTime()}</span>
                    <button onClick={() => setIsActive(!isActive)} className="p-2 rounded-full bg-[var(--theme-primary-light)] text-[var(--theme-primary)] hover:bg-opacity-50 transition-all">
                        {isActive ? <PauseIcon /> : <PlayIcon />}
                    </button>
                    <button onClick={() => { setTime(0); setIsActive(false); }} className="p-2 rounded-full bg-slate-200 text-slate-600 hover:bg-slate-300 transition-all">
                        <ResetIcon />
                    </button>
                    <div className="border-l h-6 border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-600 mx-1"></div>
                    <button
                        onClick={() => setIsThemeOpen(!isThemeOpen)}
                        className="p-2 rounded-full text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-200 bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-700 hover:bg-slate-200 dark:hover:bg-slate-600 transition-colors"
                        title="Change Theme"
                    >
                        <PaletteIcon />
                    </button>

                    {isThemeOpen && (
                        <div className="absolute left-1/2 top-full -translate-x-1/2 mt-2 w-48 bg-[var(--theme-card-bg)] rounded-lg shadow-2xl p-4 z-50">
                            <p className="text-xs font-semibold text-center mb-3 text-[var(--theme-text)] opacity-70 tracking-wider">CHOOSE THEME</p>
                            <div className="grid grid-cols-4 gap-3">
                                {Object.entries(themes).map(([key, theme]) => {
                                    const isDark = theme.name.includes('(D)');
                                    const style = {
                                        background: isDark
                                            ? `linear-gradient(90deg, #2d3748 50%, ${theme.previewColor} 50%)`
                                            : theme.previewColor,
                                        boxShadow: 'inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(0,0,0,0.1)'
                                    };
                                    return (
                                        <button
                                            key={key}
                                            onClick={() => {
                                                setCurrentTheme(key);
                                                setIsThemeOpen(false);
                                            }}
                                            className="w-8 h-8 rounded-full transition-transform hover:scale-110 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-offset-2 focus:ring-[var(--theme-primary)]"
                                            style={style}
                                            title={theme.name}
                                        />
                                    );
                                })}
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    )}
                </div>
            );
        };
        
        // --- NAVIGATION COMPONENT ---
        const YearNavigation = ({ sections, selectedYear, setSelectedYear }) => {
            const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
            const navRef = useRef(null);
            
            useEffect(() => {
                const handleClickOutside = (event) => {
                    if (navRef.current && !navRef.current.contains(event.target)) {
                        setIsOpen(false);
                    }
                };
                document.addEventListener("mousedown", handleClickOutside);
                return () => {
                    document.removeEventListener("mousedown", handleClickOutside);
                };
            }, [navRef]);
            
            const sectionOrder = ['2025', '2024', '2023', '2022', '2021', '2020', '2019'];

            return (
                <div ref={navRef} className="relative inline-block text-left">
                    <div>
                        <button
    type="button"
    style={{
        display: 'flex',
        alignItems: 'center',
        gap: '0.5rem',
        padding: '0.75rem 1.5rem',
        borderRadius: '9999px',
        fontWeight: 600,
        fontSize: '1rem',
        color: 'white',
        background: 'var(--theme-primary)',
        boxShadow: isOpen ? '0 8px 24px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.18)' : '0 4px 14px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.12)',
        transition: 'all 0.3s',
        outline: 'none',
        border: 'none',
        transform: isOpen ? 'translateY(-2px)' : 'none',
        cursor: 'pointer',
    }}
    onMouseOver={e => {
        e.currentTarget.style.boxShadow = '0 8px 24px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.18)';
        e.currentTarget.style.transform = 'translateY(-2px)';
    }}
    onMouseOut={e => {
        e.currentTarget.style.boxShadow = '0 4px 14px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.12)';
        e.currentTarget.style.transform = 'none';
    }}
    onClick={() => setIsOpen(!isOpen)}
>
    {sections[selectedYear]}
    <ChevronDownIcon />
</button>
                    </div>

                    {isOpen && (
                        <div
    style={{
        position: 'absolute',
        right: 0,
        marginTop: '0.5rem',
        width: 'auto',
        minWidth: '17rem',
        borderRadius: '0.5rem',
        boxShadow: '0 8px 24px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.18)',
        background: 'var(--theme-card-bg)',
        zIndex: 20,
        border: '1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05)',
        whiteSpace: 'nowrap',
    }}
>
    <div style={{ padding: '0.25rem 0' }} role="menu" aria-orientation="vertical" aria-labelledby="options-menu">
        {sectionOrder.map(year => (
            <a
                key={year}
                href={`#year-${year}`}
                style={{
                    display: 'block',
                    padding: '0.5rem 1rem',
                    fontSize: '0.95rem',
                    color: 'var(--theme-text)',
                    borderRadius: '0.375rem',
                    textDecoration: 'none',
                    transition: 'background 0.2s, color 0.2s',
                    whiteSpace: 'nowrap',
                }}
                role="menuitem"
                onClick={() => {
                    setSelectedYear(year);
                    setIsOpen(false);
                }}
                onMouseOver={e => {
                    e.currentTarget.style.background = '#f3f4f6';
                    e.currentTarget.style.color = '#111827';
                }}
                onMouseOut={e => {
                    e.currentTarget.style.background = 'transparent';
                    e.currentTarget.style.color = 'var(--theme-text)';
                }}
            >
                {sections[year]}
            </a>
        ))}
    </div>
</div>
                    )}
                </div>
            );
        };


        // --- BACK TO TOP BUTTON COMPONENT ---
        const BackToTopButton = () => {
            const [isVisible, setIsVisible] = useState(false);
            const toggleVisibility = () => {
                if (window.pageYOffset > 300) {
                    setIsVisible(true);
                } else {
                    setIsVisible(false);
                }
            };
            const scrollToTop = () => {
                window.scrollTo({
                    top: 0,
                    behavior: 'smooth'
                });
            };
            useEffect(() => {
                window.addEventListener('scroll', toggleVisibility);
                return () => {
                    window.removeEventListener('scroll', toggleVisibility);
                };
            }, []);
            return (
                <button
                    className={`fixed bottom-6 right-6 p-3 rounded-full text-white shadow-lg transition-opacity duration-300 ${isVisible ? 'opacity-100' : 'opacity-0'}`}
                    style={{ backgroundColor: 'var(--theme-primary)'}}
                    onClick={scrollToTop}
                    aria-label="Go to top"
                >
                    <ArrowUpIcon />
                </button>
            );
        };

        // --- QUESTION CARD COMPONENT ---
        const QuestionCard = ({ item }) => {
            const [isAnswerVisible, setIsAnswerVisible] = useState(false);
            const [isHintVisible, setIsHintVisible] = useState(false);
            const [selectedOption, setSelectedOption] = useState(null);
            const [selectionStatus, setSelectionStatus] = useState(null);

            const handleOptionClick = (option) => {
                if (selectionStatus) return;
                setSelectedOption(option);
                setSelectionStatus(option === item.answer ? 'correct' : 'incorrect');
            };
            const toggleAnswer = () => setIsAnswerVisible(!isAnswerVisible);
            const toggleHint = () => setIsHintVisible(!isHintVisible);
            const getOptionClass = (option) => {
                const isDark = document.documentElement.style.getPropertyValue('--theme-bg').startsWith('#1');
                if (selectionStatus) {
                    if (option === item.answer) return 'bg-green-100 dark:bg-green-900/50 border-green-500 text-green-800 dark:text-green-300 animate-pulse';
                    if (option === selectedOption) return 'bg-red-100 dark:bg-red-900/50 border-red-500 text-red-800 dark:text-red-300';
                    return isDark ? 'cursor-not-allowed text-slate-500 border-slate-700' : 'cursor-not-allowed text-slate-500 border-slate-200';
                }
                return isDark ? 'border-slate-600 hover:bg-slate-700/50 hover:border-slate-500' : 'border-slate-300 hover:bg-slate-100/50 hover:border-slate-400';
            };
            return (
                <div className="bg-[var(--theme-card-bg)] rounded-2xl p-4 sm:p-6 md:p-8 mb-6 shadow-lg transition-all duration-300 overflow-hidden" style={{boxShadow: '0 10px 15px -3px var(--theme-card-shadow), 0 4px 6px -4px var(--theme-card-shadow)'}}>
                    <div className="flex justify-between items-start gap-4">
                        <p className="font-semibold text-[var(--theme-text)] flex-1 text-base md:text-lg break-words" dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: item.question }}></p>
                        {item.hint && <button onClick={toggleHint} className="p-2 rounded-full text-yellow-500 hover:text-yellow-600 hover:bg-yellow-100 transition-colors duration-200 flex-shrink-0" title="Show Hint"><HintIcon /></button>}
                    </div>
                    
                    <div className={`transition-all duration-500 ease-in-out grid ${isHintVisible ? 'grid-rows-[1fr] opacity-100' : 'grid-rows-[0fr] opacity-0'}`}>
                        <div className="overflow-hidden">
                            <div className="mt-3 p-3 bg-[var(--theme-hint-bg)] border border-dashed rounded-lg text-sm text-[var(--theme-hint-text)]" style={{borderColor: 'var(--theme-hint-border)'}}><strong>Hint:</strong> {item.hint}</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    {item.options && (
                        <div className="mt-4 space-y-3">
                            {item.options.map((option, index) => (
                                <button
    key={index}
    onClick={() => handleOptionClick(option)}
    disabled={!!selectionStatus}
    style={{
        width: '100%',
        textAlign: 'left',
        padding: '0.75rem',
        borderWidth: 2,
        borderStyle: 'solid',
        borderRadius: '0.5rem',
        transition: 'all 0.3s',
        fontSize: '1rem',
        fontWeight: 500,
        ...(getOptionClass(option) === 'bg-green-100 dark:bg-green-900/50 border-green-500 text-green-800 dark:text-green-300 animate-pulse' && {
            background: '#bbf7d0',
            borderColor: '#22c55e',
            color: '#166534',
            animation: 'pulse 1s infinite',
        }),
        ...(getOptionClass(option) === 'bg-red-100 dark:bg-red-900/50 border-red-500 text-red-800 dark:text-red-300' && {
            background: '#fecaca',
            borderColor: '#ef4444',
            color: '#991b1b',
        }),
        ...(getOptionClass(option) === 'cursor-not-allowed text-slate-500 border-slate-700' && {
            color: '#64748b',
            borderColor: '#334155',
            cursor: 'not-allowed',
        }),
        ...(getOptionClass(option) === 'cursor-not-allowed text-slate-500 border-slate-200' && {
            color: '#64748b',
            borderColor: '#e5e7eb',
            cursor: 'not-allowed',
        }),
        ...(getOptionClass(option) === 'border-slate-600 hover:bg-slate-700/50 hover:border-slate-500' && {
            background: 'var(--theme-card-bg)',
            color: 'var(--theme-text)',
            borderColor: '#475569',
        }),
        ...(getOptionClass(option) === 'border-slate-300 hover:bg-slate-100/50 hover:border-slate-400' && {
            background: 'var(--theme-card-bg)',
            color: 'var(--theme-text)',
            borderColor: '#d1d5db',
        }),
    }}
>
    {option}
</button>
                            ))}
                        </div>
                    )}
                    {selectionStatus && item.explanation && (
                        <div className="mt-4 p-4 bg-[var(--theme-hint-bg)] border-l-4 rounded-r-lg" style={{borderColor: 'var(--theme-primary)'}}>
                            <strong className="text-[var(--theme-heading)]">Explanation:</strong>
                            <div className="prose prose-sm max-w-none prose-p:my-1 text-[var(--theme-hint-text)] mt-1" dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: item.explanation }} />
                        </div>
                    )}
                    {!item.options && (
                        <>
                            <button
    onClick={toggleAnswer}
    style={{
        marginTop: '1rem',
        padding: '0.5rem 1.25rem',
        borderRadius: '9999px',
        fontWeight: 500,
        fontSize: '0.875rem',
        transition: 'all 0.2s',
        background: isAnswerVisible ? '#475569' : '#e2e8f0', // bg-slate-600 or bg-slate-200
        color: isAnswerVisible ? '#fff' : '#1e293b', // text-white or text-slate-800
        border: 'none',
        cursor: 'pointer',
        outline: 'none',
    }}
    onMouseOver={e => {
        e.currentTarget.style.background = isAnswerVisible ? '#334155' : '#cbd5e1'; // hover:bg-slate-700 or hover:bg-slate-300
    }}
    onMouseOut={e => {
        e.currentTarget.style.background = isAnswerVisible ? '#475569' : '#e2e8f0';
    }}
>
    {isAnswerVisible ? 'Hide Answer' : 'View Answer'}
</button>
                            <div className={`transition-all duration-500 ease-in-out grid ${isAnswerVisible ? 'grid-rows-[1fr] opacity-100' : 'grid-rows-[0fr] opacity-0'}`}>
                                <div className="overflow-hidden">
                                    <div className="mt-4 p-4 bg-[var(--theme-hint-bg)] border border-dashed rounded-lg" style={{borderColor: 'var(--theme-hint-border)'}}>
                                        <div className="prose prose-sm max-w-none prose-p:my-1 prose-ul:my-1 prose-ol:my-1 text-[var(--theme-hint-text)] prose-strong:text-[var(--theme-text)]">
                                            <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: item.answer }} />
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </>
                    )}
                </div>
            );
        };

        // --- MAIN APP COMPONENT ---
        function App() {
            const [currentTheme, setCurrentTheme] = useState('sunriseOrange');
            const [selectedYear, setSelectedYear] = useState('2025');

            useEffect(() => {
                const theme = themes[currentTheme];
                Object.entries(theme.cssVars).forEach(([key, value]) => {
                    document.documentElement.style.setProperty(key, value);
                });
            }, [currentTheme]);
            
            const sections = {
                '2025': 'Previous Year Questions: 2025',
                '2024': 'Previous Year Questions: 2024',
                '2023': 'Previous Year Questions: 2023',
                '2022': 'Previous Year Questions: 2022',
                '2021': 'Previous Year Questions: 2021',
                '2020': 'Previous Year Questions: 2020',
                '2019': 'Previous Year Questions: 2019',
            };
            
            const sectionOrder = ['2025', '2024', '2023', '2022', '2021', '2020', '2019'];

            return (
                <div className="bg-[var(--theme-bg)] min-h-screen font-sans text-[var(--theme-text)] p-4 sm:p-6 md:p-8 overflow-x-hidden">
                <GlobalStyles />
                    <div className="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
                        <header className="relative text-center mt-20 mb-4">
                            <h1 className="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-[var(--theme-heading)] break-words">PYQs: Current Electricity</h1>
                        </header>
                        
                        <div className="flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-center items-center gap-4 my-8">
                                <TimerAndTheme setCurrentTheme={setCurrentTheme} />
                                <YearNavigation sections={sections} selectedYear={selectedYear} setSelectedYear={setSelectedYear} />
                        </div>
                        
                        {sectionOrder.map(sectionKey => (
                            <section key={sectionKey} id={`year-${sectionKey}`} className="mb-12 scroll-mt-40">
                                <h2 className="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--theme-heading)] mb-8 pb-3 break-words" style={{ borderBottom: '4px solid var(--theme-heading-border)' }}>
                                    {sections[sectionKey]}
                                </h2>
                                <div>
                                    {worksheetData
                                        .filter(item => item.section === sectionKey)
                                        .map(item => <QuestionCard key={item.id} item={item} />)}
                                </div>
                            </section>
                        ))}
                    </div>
                    <div className="fixed bottom-6 right-6 z-30">
                         <BackToTopButton />
                    </div>
                </div>
            );
        }

export default App;

